OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026", "title": "Midpoint convexity from bounded tree potentials and path costs", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf", "family": "331", "family_title": "Reflexive midpoint convexity and diamond distortion", "abstract_html": "Four real Banach spaces defined by bounded tree potentials satisfy the averaged asymptotic midpoint bound \\(\\widehat\\delta(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1\\) for \\(0\\lt t\\lt 1\\), while none admits an asymptotically uniformly convex (AUC) renorming. On finite-height trees, the globally constrained norm equals the least additive cost of a Hilbert vector and root paths. The quadratic path and segment-start outer Hilbert sums are reflexive and asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex, and admit no equivalent AUC norm.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T=\\mathbb N^{&lt;\\omega}\\), with root \\(o\\), predecessor order \\(\\preceq\\), parent \\(s^{-}\\), and child set \\(\\operatorname{ch}(r)\\). Put \\(T^{+}=T\\setminus\\{o\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For coefficients on \\(I\\subseteq T\\), define\n  \\[\n  P_f(s)=\\sum_{\\substack{u\\in I\\\\u\\preceq s}}f_u.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The four symmetric norming sets all satisfy \\(|P_f(s)|\\le1\\) for every \\(s\\in T\\), with the following additional conditions:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n  K&amp;I&amp;\\text{additional conditions}\\\\ \\hline\n  K_s&amp;T^+&amp;\\sum_{u\\in\\operatorname{ch}(r)}|f_u|^2\\le1\\quad(r\\in T),\\\\\n  K_p&amp;T&amp;\\sum_{u\\in\\operatorname{ch}(r)}|f_u|^2\\le1\\quad(r\\in T),\\\\\n  K_a&amp;T&amp;f\\in c_{00}(T),\\quad \\sum_{u\\in A}|f_u|^2\\le1\n       \\quad(A\\text{ a finite antichain}),\\\\\n  K_g&amp;T&amp;f\\in\\ell_2(T),\\quad \\sum_{u\\in T}|f_u|^2\\le1.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n  An antichain contains no two comparable nodes.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\|x\\|_K=\\sup_{f\\in K}\\sum_{s\\in I}f_sx_s\n  \\]\n  on \\(c_{00}(I)\\), and let \\(X_K\\) be its completion.</p><p class=\"li\">For an infinite-dimensional Banach space with norm \\(N\\), let \\(\\operatorname{cof}(X)\\) be its closed finite-codimensional subspaces and define\n  \\[\n  \\widehat\\delta_N(t)=\n  \\inf_{N(x)=1}\\sup_{F\\in\\operatorname{cof}(X)}\n  \\inf_{\\substack{y\\in F\\\\N(y)\\ge1}}\n  \\left(\\frac{N(x+ty)+N(x-ty)}2-1\\right),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\overline\\delta_N(t)=\n  \\inf_{N(x)=1}\\sup_{F\\in\\operatorname{cof}(X)}\n  \\inf_{\\substack{y\\in F\\\\N(y)=1}}\n  \\bigl(N(x+ty)-1\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  A norm is asymptotically uniformly convex, abbreviated AUC, when \\(\\overline\\delta_N(t)&gt;0\\) for every \\(t&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(X_K\\) is a separable infinite-dimensional real Banach space, and\n\\[\n\\widehat\\delta_{X_K}(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1\n\\qquad(0&lt;t&lt;1).\n\\]\nNone of these four spaces admits an equivalent AUC norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each norming set is symmetric, its coefficients are bounded by one, and every signed singleton is a test. Thus the formula defines a norm, \\(\\|e_s\\|_K=1\\), coordinate evaluations are continuous, and tests extend continuously. Their supremum remains norming by density and its \\(1\\)-Lipschitz property. Finite rational arrays are dense, and the coordinate vectors are independent.</p><p>For a finite unit center \\(x\\) supported in \\(D\\), apply Proposition 1 to every \\(y\\in F_D\\) of norm at least one, with\n\\[\n\\theta=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4+t^2}}&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe right side becomes \\(\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}\\). For an arbitrary unit center \\(x_0\\), choose a finite unit \\(x\\) within \\(\\varepsilon\\). The average loses at most \\(\\varepsilon\\), uniformly over the same tail space. The subspace may depend on \\(\\varepsilon\\); let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\) before taking the infimum over centers. This proves the modulus estimate. The paper also records\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1\n=\\frac{t^2}{4(\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}+1)}\\ge t^2/16.\n\\]\nNo optimality or equality of the four norms is asserted.</p><p>For the obstruction, put\n\\[\np_s=\\sum_{\\substack{u\\in I\\\\u\\preceq s}}e_u.\n\\]\nThe path condition and a singleton test give \\(\\|p_s\\|_K=1\\). In the rootless case start at a fixed nonroot \\(v\\), using \\(p_{v^\\frown s}\\). In all four models,\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_{n\\in A}a_ne_{s^\\frown n}\\right\\|_K\n\\le\\left(\\sum_{n\\in A}|a_n|^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\]\nfor finite \\(A\\): use sibling, antichain, or global constraints as appropriate. For \\(\\phi\\in X_K^*\\), choose \\(a_n=\\phi(e_{s^\\frown n})\\), obtaining\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\in A}|\\phi(e_{s^\\frown n})|^2\\le\\|\\phi\\|^2.\n\\]\nThus the child increments are weakly null and have norm one. Proposition 2 applies with \\(m=M=c=1\\), giving\n\\[\n\\overline\\delta_N(\\alpha/(2\\beta))=0\n\\]\nfor every equivalent norm.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(T=\\mathbb N^{&lt;\\omega}\\), identify each \\(s\\in T^+\\) with the edge from \\(s^-\\) to \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B=\n  \\left\\{b:T\\to[-1,1]:\n  \\sum_{w\\in\\operatorname{ch}(v)}|b_w-b_v|^2\\le1\n  \\quad(v\\in T)\\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  The root value \\(b_o\\) is free; there is no root coordinate or dummy edge.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  f_b(x)=\\sum_{s\\in T^+}x_s(b_s-b_{s^-}),\n  \\qquad\n  \\|x\\|_e=\\sup_{b\\in\\mathcal B}f_b(x),\n  \\]\n  and let \\(X_e\\) be the completion of \\(c_{00}(T^+)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat\\delta_{\\|\\cdot\\|_e}(t)\n\\ge\\frac{\\sqrt{1+t^2/576}-1}{4}.\n\\]\nMoreover, if\n\\[\n\\alpha\\|\\cdot\\|_e\\le N\\le\\beta\\|\\cdot\\|_e,\n\\qquad 0&lt;\\alpha\\le\\beta&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\overline\\delta_N\\!\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{4\\beta}\\right)=0.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(X_e\\) admits no equivalent AUC norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every increment of an admissible potential has magnitude at most one. The potential equal to one on a subtree and zero elsewhere has one nonzero increment. Hence\n\\[\n|x_s|\\le\\|x\\|_e\\le\\sum_u|x_u|,\\qquad\\|e_s\\|_e=1.\n\\]\nTests extend and remain norming.</p><p>For a finite initial subtree \\(D\\), freeze a potential after its last node in \\(D\\). This preserves admissibility, makes \\(P_D\\) contractive, and gives\n\\[\nF_D=\\ker P_D=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{e_s:s\\notin D\\}.\n\\]\nFinite labels in \\(D\\) form a compact admissible set, so a finite vector supported there has a norming potential \\(b^0\\) frozen outside \\(D\\).</p><p>Fix a finite unit center \\(x\\), and finite unit \\(y\\in F_D\\). Then\n\\[\nf_{b^0}(x)=1,\\qquad f_{b^0}(y)=0,\\qquad\n\\|x\\pm ty\\|_e\\ge1.\n\\]\nLet \\(E\\) be the first exits, and write \\(y=y_E+y_L\\).</p><p>First suppose \\(|f_c(y_E)|\\ge1/2\\) for some \\(c\\in\\mathcal B\\). Put\n\\[\nL_v=\\left(\\sum_{r\\in\\operatorname{ch}(v)\\setminus D}|y_r|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nSibling Cauchy–Schwarz gives \\(\\sum_{v\\in D}L_v\\ge1/2\\). Choose the inward sign \\(\\sigma_v\\), and define\n\\[\nL_{v,\\epsilon}=\n\\left(\\sum_{\\substack{r\\in\\operatorname{ch}(v)\\setminus D\\\\\n\\epsilon\\sigma_vy_r&gt;0}}|y_r|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(L_{v,1}+L_{v,-1}\\ge L_v\\), one common sign \\(\\epsilon\\) has\n\\(\\sum_vL_{v,\\epsilon}\\ge1/4\\).\nScale head labels by \\(1-h^2\\); at selected exits assign jumps\n\\[\nd_r=\\epsilon h y_r/L_{v,\\epsilon},\n\\]\nzero elsewhere, and freeze descendants. Each jump points inward and has magnitude at most \\(h\\); crossing zero leaves magnitude at most \\(h&lt;1\\). At each head parent the total square sum is at most\n\\((1-h^2)^2+h^2\\le1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_e\\ge1-h^2+th/4.\n\\]\nTaking \\(h=t/8\\) gives \\(1+t^2/64\\).</p><p>Otherwise choose \\(c\\in\\mathcal B\\) with \\(f_c(y)&gt;5/6\\); then \\(f_c(y_L)&gt;1/3\\).\nUse planar labels satisfying\n\\[\n|B_v|_2\\le1,\\qquad\n\\sum_{w\\in\\operatorname{ch}(v)}|B_w-B_v|_2^2\\le1.\n\\]\nEvery scalar projection is admissible, so\n\\[\nA_Bz=\\sum_sz_s(B_s-B_{s^-})\n\\]\nis contractive into \\(\\mathbb R^2\\).</p><p>On \\(D\\), set \\(B_v=(b_v^0,0)\\). In the subtree of a first exit \\(r\\), set\n\\[\nu=b^0_{r^-},\\qquad k=1/8,\\qquad\nz_v=k(c_v-c_r),\\qquad\nB_v=(u\\sqrt{1-z_v^2},z_v).\n\\]\nFirst-exit increments vanish, \\(|z_v|\\le1/4\\), and \\(|B_v|_2\\le1\\). The map\n\\[\nH(a)=\\bigl(u\\sqrt{1-k^2(a-c_r)^2},\\,k(a-c_r)\\bigr)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|H'(a)|_2^2\n=k^2+\\frac{u^2k^4(a-c_r)^2}{1-k^2(a-c_r)^2}\n\\le\\frac{k^2}{1-4k^2}=\\frac1{60}.\n\\]\nIt is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz, so all deeper sibling constraints hold; boundary constraints retain the old head increments and zero exits.\nNow \\(A_Bx=(1,0)\\), and the second coordinate of \\(A_By\\) exceeds \\(1/24\\). The parallelogram identity gives\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_e^2\\ge1+t^2/576.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(G(t)=\\sqrt{1+t^2/576}-1\\). Both cases give maximum at least \\(1+G(t)\\), while both endpoints are at least one, so their average is at least \\(1+G(t)/2\\). Density extends this to unit tails. Even convexity in the radius extends it to tails of norm at least one. Approximate an arbitrary unit center within \\(G(t)/4\\); the average loses at most that amount, proving the stated bound.</p><p>For nonroot \\(s\\), let \\(p_s\\) be its edge-path sum. Telescoping gives\n\\[\n1\\le\\|p_s\\|_e\\le2.\n\\]\nSibling constraints give\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_na_ne_{s^\\frown n}\\right\\|_e\n\\le\\left(\\sum_n|a_n|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nApplying a dual functional as in Theorem 1 proves weak nullity of the unit child increments. Start at a fixed nonroot node and apply Proposition 2 with \\(m=1,M=2,c=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Proposition 5.1 and Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\ge1\\), let \\(T_n=\\mathbb N^{\\le n}\\), including its root \\(o\\), and \\(H_n=\\ell_2(T_n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  b_s=\\sum_{r\\preceq s}e_r,\\qquad\n  B_n\\mu=\\sum_{s\\in T_n}\\mu_sb_s,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  L_n(x)=\\inf_{x=h+B_n\\mu}\\bigl(\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_1\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  p_n(f)=\\max\\left\\{\\|f\\|_2,\\sup_{s\\in T_n}\n                 \\left|\\sum_{r\\preceq s}f_r\\right|\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_n=(H_n,L_n)\\) and\n  \\[\n  X=\\left(\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}X_n\\right)_{\\ell_2},\n  \\qquad\n  \\|x\\|^2=\\sum_nL_n(x_n)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An initial set contains every predecessor of each of its nodes. A finite head is a finite initial subset of the disjoint union of the \\(T_n\\). Write \\(P_A\\) for coordinate restriction to \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact duality formula is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x\\|_2}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\\le L_n(x)\\le\\|x\\|_2,\n\\qquad\nL_n(x)=\\sup_{p_n(f)\\le1}\\langle f,x\\rangle.\n\\]\nEvery coordinate satisfies \\(|x_s|\\le L_n(x)\\), and \\(X_n^*\\) is \\(H_n\\), under the Hilbert pairing, with norm \\(p_n\\). Thus \\(L_n\\) is exactly the globally constrained potential norm on \\(T_n\\).</p><p>The space \\(X\\) is reflexive, has separable dual, and\n\\[\nX^*=\\left(\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}(H_n,p_n)\\right)_{\\ell_2},\n\\qquad\nf(x)=\\sum_n\\langle f_n,x_n\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\|f\\|_*^2=\\sum_np_n(f_n)^2.\n\\]\nEvery coordinate vector \\(e_{n,s}\\) and root path \\(b_{n,s}\\) has norm one. For fixed \\(n\\) and nonterminal \\(s\\), the children \\(e_{n,s^\\frown j}\\) are weakly null.</p><p>If \\(\\alpha\\|\\cdot\\|\\le N\\le\\beta\\|\\cdot\\|\\), then\n\\[\n\\overline\\delta_N(t)=0\\qquad(0&lt;t\\le\\alpha/\\beta).\n\\]\nConsequently \\(X\\) admits no equivalent AUC norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 6 and 7 give the exact norm, duality, completeness, reflexivity, separable dual, finite-head properties, unit paths, and weakly null children.</p><p>For the sharper obstruction scale, suppose\n\\[\nd=\\overline\\delta_N(t)&gt;0,\\qquad0&lt;t\\le\\alpha/\\beta.\n\\]\nChoose \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;d/2\\). At every \\(N\\)-unit center \\(u\\), choose finite-codimensional \\(F\\) with\n\\(N(u+tv)\\ge1+2\\eta\\) for \\(v\\in S_{N,F}\\). Convexity gives the same gain for directions in \\(F\\) of norm at least \\(t\\).</p><p>At an internal path \\(b_{n,s}\\), put\n\\[\nu=b_{n,s}/N(b_{n,s}),\\qquad\nz_j=e_{n,s^\\frown j}/N(b_{n,s}).\n\\]\nThen \\(z_j\\to0\\) weakly and \\(N(z_j)\\ge\\alpha/\\beta\\ge t\\). Approximate \\(z_j\\) by \\(v_j\\in F\\). If \\(N(v_j)&lt;t\\), replace it by \\(tv_j/N(v_j)\\); the extra change is at most \\(N(v_j-z_j)\\). Thus a sufficiently large child satisfies\n\\[\nN(b_{n,s^\\frown j})\\ge(1+\\eta)N(b_{n,s}).\n\\]\nIterating through height \\(n\\) yields\n\\(\\alpha(1+\\eta)^n\\le\\beta\\), impossible for all \\(n\\). Nonnegativity of the modulus gives equality to zero. Taking \\(\\eta=d/4\\) yields the recorded growth factor \\(1+d/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Theorem 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the linear path space \\(X\\), norms \\(L_n\\), and finite-head projections defined in Theorem 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the linear path space \\(X\\), norms \\(L_n\\), and finite-head projections defined in Theorem 3.</p><p>If \\(P_Ax=x\\) and \\(P_Ay=0\\) for a finite head \\(A\\), then, for every \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\|x+y\\|,\\|x-y\\|\\}\n\\ge\\frac{\\|x\\|+c\\|y\\|/2}{\\sqrt{1+4c^2}}.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\|x+y\\|,\\|x-y\\|\\}\n\\ge\\sqrt{\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2/16}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose norming dual vectors \\(h,g\\) for \\(x,y\\), of norm at most one, and replace \\(h\\) by \\(P_Ah\\). Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\nr_1(y)-r_2(y)=\\|y\\|.\n\\]\nOne index \\(l\\) has \\(|r_l(y)|\\ge\\|y\\|/2\\). Choose \\(\\epsilon\\) with\n\\(\\epsilon r_l(y)\\ge\\|y\\|/2\\). Since \\(h(y)=r_l(x)=0\\), evaluation of \\(h+cr_l\\) on \\(x+\\epsilon y\\) gives the asserted quotient bound.</p><p>Its supremum over \\(c&gt;0\\) is\n\\(\\sqrt{\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2/16}\\), by two-dimensional Cauchy–Schwarz; endpoint cases follow by a limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Theorem 5.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(x\\in X\\) and let \\((y_j)\\) be a bounded weakly null sequence.", "statement": "<p>In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(x\\in X\\) and let \\((y_j)\\) be a bounded weakly null sequence. If\n\\[\nL=\\liminf_j\\|y_j\\|,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\liminf_j\\max\\{\\|x+y_j\\|,\\|x-y_j\\|\\}\n\\ge\\sqrt{\\|x\\|^2+L^2/16}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and choose a finite head \\(A\\) with \\(\\|x-P_Ax\\|\\le\\eta\\). Put \\(z_j=(I-P_A)y_j\\). Finite rank and weak nullity give\n\\[\n\\|P_Ay_j\\|\\to0,\\qquad\n\\bigl|\\|z_j\\|-\\|y_j\\|\\bigr|\\le\\|P_Ay_j\\|.\n\\]\nApply Theorem 4 to \\(P_Ax,z_j\\):\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm y_j\\|\n\\ge\\sqrt{\\|P_Ax\\|^2+\\|z_j\\|^2/16}\n-\\eta-\\|P_Ay_j\\|.\n\\]\nKeep this head fixed while taking the lower limit. Then let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\), choosing heads along the strong exhaustion.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 6", "orig": "Theorem 5.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the linear path model of Theorem 3, and put \\(C=6(\\sqrt2+1)\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the linear path model of Theorem 3, and put \\(C=6(\\sqrt2+1)\\).</p><p>In one component, if\n\\[\nP_Ax=x,\\qquad P_Ay=0,\\qquad L_n(x\\pm y)\\le1,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nL_n(y)\\le C\\sqrt{1-L_n(x)}.\n\\]\nFor arbitrary \\(x,y\\) with \\(P_Ax=x\\), \\(P_Ay=0\\), setting\n\\[\n\\beta=\\max\\{L_n(x+y),L_n(x-y)\\},\\qquad r=L_n(x),\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nL_n(y)^2\\le C^2\\beta(\\beta-r)\\le C^2(\\beta^2-r^2).\n\\]\nConsequently, for every finite-head projection \\(P\\) on \\(X\\),\n\\[\n\\|y\\|^2\\le C^2\\bigl(\\|x+y\\|^2+\\|x-y\\|^2-2\\|x\\|^2\\bigr)\n\\qquad(Px=x,\\ Py=0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work first in one component. Set \\(r=L_n(x)\\), and choose a supported norming functional \\(g=gP_A\\), with \\(g(x)=r\\); use zero for \\(x=0\\). Choose\n\\[\nx\\pm y=h_\\pm+B_n\\alpha_\\pm\n\\]\nof costs at most \\(1+\\eta\\), and put \\(d=1+\\eta-r\\). Subtract the value of \\(g\\) from each cost:\n\\[\n\\|h_\\pm\\|_2-g(h_\\pm)+\n\\sum_t\\bigl(|\\alpha_\\pm(t)|-\\alpha_\\pm(t)g(b_t)\\bigr)\\le d.\n\\]\nEvery term is nonnegative. With \\(U=T_n\\setminus A\\),\n\\[\n\\|h_\\pm|_U\\|_2\\le\\sqrt{2(1+\\eta)d}.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(|g(h_\\pm)|\\le\\|P_Ah_\\pm\\|_2\\), and factoring the difference of squares bounds the other factor by \\(2(1+\\eta)\\).\nCoefficients with \\(\\alpha_\\pm(t)g(b_t)\\le0\\) have total mass at most \\(d\\).</p><p>Retain the other coefficients ending in \\(U\\), with forest outputs \\(v_\\pm\\). Then\n\\[\ny|_U=v_++w_+,\\qquad\n-y|_U=v_-+w_-,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nL_U(w_\\pm)\\le K:=\\sqrt{2(1+\\eta)d}+d.\n\\]\nOn each complementary tree, \\(g(b_t)\\) is constant; both retained arrays have its sign, and neither is retained if that value is zero. A whole-tree sign change makes them nonnegative. Proposition 9 yields\n\\[\nL_U(v_+)\\le L_U(v_++v_-)=L_U(w_++w_-)\\le2K.\n\\]\nHence \\(L_U(y|_U)\\le3K\\), and \\(L_n(y)\\le6K\\). Let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) and use \\(1-r\\le\\sqrt{1-r}\\).</p><p>Scaling gives \\(L_n(y)^2\\le C^2\\beta(\\beta-r)\\), with the zero case immediate. Contractivity gives \\(r\\le\\beta\\), hence the second bound. In each component both endpoint norms are at least its center norm; the maximum squared excess is bounded by the sum of the two squared excesses. Sum over components, including empty heads.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 7", "orig": "Theorem 5.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the linear path model of Theorem 3.", "statement": "<p>Use the linear path model of Theorem 3.</p><p>If \\(0\\le\\varepsilon\\le1\\) and, in one component,\n\\[\nP_Ax=x,\\qquad P_Ay=0,\\qquad L_n(x\\pm y)\\le1,\\qquad\nL_n(x)\\ge1-\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nL_n(y)\\le3\\sqrt{2\\varepsilon}+8\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nFurthermore, if finite-head projections \\(P_j\\) and vectors \\(x_j,y_j\\in X\\) satisfy\n\\[\nP_jx_j=x_j,\\qquad P_jy_j=0,\\qquad\n\\|x_j\\pm y_j\\|\\le1,\\qquad \\|x_j\\|\\to1,\n\\]\nthen \\(\\|y_j\\|\\to0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(g=gP_A\\) norming \\(x\\), and finite path representations of both endpoints with costs at most \\(1+\\eta\\). Put\n\\[\nd=1+\\eta-L_n(x)\\le\\varepsilon+\\eta.\n\\]\nThe deficit calculation in Theorem 6 gives Hilbert tail norms\n\\(K_\\pm\\le\\sqrt{2(1+\\eta)d}\\), and bad coefficient masses \\(B_\\pm\\le d\\), where bad means \\(\\alpha_\\pm(t)g(b_t)\\le0\\).</p><p>In a complementary tree with common value \\(c=g(b_t)\\ne0\\), orient by \\(\\varsigma=\\operatorname{sign}(c)\\). Good coefficients have sign \\(\\varsigma\\), and bad nonzero coefficients the opposite sign. Apply Proposition 10 to combined good mass \\(\\mu\\) and combined bad mass \\(\\nu\\), retaining the originating endpoint labels. If \\(G(v),W(v)\\) are their descendant masses, the vanishing average tail gives\n\\[\nh_+(v)+h_-(v)+\\varsigma(G(v)-W(v))=0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n(G(v)-W(v))_+\\le|h_+(v)+h_-(v)|.\n\\]\nThe assertion is immediate also in trees with no good mass.</p><p>Remove the quota-selected good mass \\(\\rho\\). The remaining good tail in either representation is pointwise bounded by\n\\[\nG(v)-\\rho(K_v)\\le(G(v)-W(v))_+,\n\\]\nso its Hilbert norm is at most \\(K_++K_-\\). Removed good mass is at most \\(B_++B_-\\), and each complementary path costs at most two. The plus tail therefore satisfies\n\\[\nL_n(y)\\le2K_++K_-+2B_++2(B_++B_-)\n\\le3\\sqrt{2(1+\\eta)d}+8d.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>For the outer-sum assertion, set\n\\[\na^\\pm_{j,n}=L_n(x_{j,n}\\pm y_{j,n}),\\quad\nr_{j,n}=L_n(x_{j,n}),\\quad\nc_{j,n}=(a^+_{j,n}+a^-_{j,n})/2,\n\\]\nand \\(a_{j,n}=\\max(a^+_{j,n},a^-_{j,n})\\).\nConvexity gives \\(c_{j,n}\\ge r_{j,n}\\), so \\(\\|c_j\\|_2\\to1\\). The Hilbert parallelogram identity gives\n\\[\n\\|a_j^+-a_j^-\\|_2^2\n\\le4(1-\\|x_j\\|^2)\\to0.\n\\]\nSince \\(a_j=c_j+|a_j^+-a_j^-|/2\\), also \\(\\|a_j\\|_2\\to1\\). Ignore zero coordinates, and put\n\\(\\delta_{j,n}=1-r_{j,n}/a_{j,n}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_na_{j,n}^2\\delta_{j,n}\n\\le\\sum_n(a_{j,n}^2-r_{j,n}^2)\\to0.\n\\]\nThe component estimate gives\n\\[\nL_n(y_{j,n})/a_{j,n}\\le3\\sqrt{2\\delta_{j,n}}+8\\delta_{j,n},\n\\]\nand the same ratio is at most one. For fixed \\(\\tau&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\|y_j\\|^2\\le\n(3\\sqrt{2\\tau}+8\\tau)^2\\sum_na_{j,n}^2+\n\\tau^{-1}\\sum_na_{j,n}^2\\delta_{j,n}.\n\\]\nLet \\(j\\to\\infty\\), then \\(\\tau\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 8", "orig": "Theorem 5.10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, for every finite-head projection \\(P\\), \\( \\|y\\|^2\\le242\\bigl(\\|x+y\\|^2+\\|x-y\\|^2-2\\|x\\|^2\\bigr) \\qquad(Px=x,\\ Py=0). \\) The same assertion holds in each component, with …", "statement": "<p>In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, for every finite-head projection \\(P\\),\n\\[\n\\|y\\|^2\\le242\\bigl(\\|x+y\\|^2+\\|x-y\\|^2-2\\|x\\|^2\\bigr)\n\\qquad(Px=x,\\ Py=0).\n\\]\nThe same assertion holds in each component, with \\(L_n\\) in place of \\(\\|\\cdot\\|\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In one component set \\(r=L_n(x)\\), \\(M_\\pm=L_n(x\\pm y)\\), so \\(M_\\pm\\ge r\\). Choose a supported norming \\(g\\), finite representations of costs at most \\(M_\\pm+\\eta\\), and \\(d_\\pm=M_\\pm+\\eta-r\\).</p><p>Call a coefficient good when\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sign}(\\alpha_\\pm(t))g(b_t)&gt;1/2,\n\\]\nand bad otherwise. The deficit calculation gives\n\\[\nB_\\pm\\le2d_\\pm,\\qquad\nK_\\pm\\le\\sqrt{2(M_\\pm+\\eta)d_\\pm}.\n\\]\nOn a complementary tree with \\(|c|=|g(b_t)|&gt;1/2\\), good coefficients have sign \\(\\varsigma=\\operatorname{sign}(c)\\), and bad nonzero coefficients have the opposite sign. If \\(G_\\pm,W\\) are the corresponding descendant masses,\n\\[\nh_+(v)+h_-(v)+\\varsigma(G_+(v)+G_-(v)-W(v))=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(G=G_+\\),\n\\[\n(G-W)_+\\le|h_++h_-|.\n\\]\nTrees with \\(|c|\\le1/2\\) have no good mass.</p><p>Proposition 10 removes at most \\(B_++B_-\\) from the plus good coefficients. The remaining good tail has Hilbert norm at most \\(K_++K_-\\), and removed complementary paths cost at most \\(2(B_++B_-)\\). Hence\n\\[\nL_n(y)\\le2(K_++K_-)+4(B_++B_-)\n\\le11\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}}\\sqrt{(M_\\sigma+\\eta)d_\\sigma},\n\\]\nusing \\(d_\\sigma\\le M_\\sigma+\\eta\\) and \\(2\\sqrt2+8&lt;11\\).\nSquare, paying a factor two, and use\n\\[\n(M_\\sigma+\\eta)d_\\sigma\\le(M_\\sigma+\\eta)^2-r^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\), obtaining \\(2\\cdot11^2=242\\), then sum over components. Empty heads have zero centers.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 9", "orig": "Theorem 5.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(A\\subseteq A'\\) be finite ancestor-closed sets and put \\(E=A'\\setminus A\\).", "statement": "<p>In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(A\\subseteq A'\\) be finite ancestor-closed sets and put \\(E=A'\\setminus A\\). If\n\\[\nP_Ax=x,\\qquad P_{A'}y=y,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\|P_Ey\\|^2\\le400\n\\left(\\frac{\\|x+y\\|^2+\\|x-y\\|^2}{2}-\\|x\\|^2\\right).\n\\]\nThere is no condition on \\(P_Ay\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(x^s=x+sy\\), \\(s\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\), and use bars for sign averages. For \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\), choose, in each nonzero component,\n\\[\nx_n^s=z_n^s+B_n\\alpha_n^s,\\qquad\nr_n^s=\\|z_n^s\\|_2+M_n^s\\le(1+\\lambda)L_n(x_n^s),\n\\quad M_n^s=\\|\\alpha_n^s\\|_1.\n\\]\nUse zero decompositions in zero components. Project representations to \\(A'\\), truncating Hilbert vectors and pushing endpoints to their last ancestor there. Both costs decrease.</p><p>Let \\(\\pi_A(t)\\) be the last ancestor in \\(A\\), or \\(\\perp\\). In each fibre choose\n\\[\nL_b=\\sum_{\\pi_A(t)=b}\\bar\\alpha_n(t),\n\\qquad l_bL_b=|L_b|,\\quad l_b\\in\\{-1,1\\}.\n\\]\nOn labeled pairs \\((s,t)\\), put\n\\[\nm_n(s,t)=|\\alpha_n^s(t)|/2,\n\\]\nand let \\(h(s,t)\\) be the coefficient sign. Define wrong-sign mass\n\\[\nW_n=\\sum_{\\{(s,t):h(s,t)\\ne l_{\\pi_A(t)}\\}}m_n(s,t).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\bar M_n-\\sum_b|L_b|=2W_n\\), including the dummy fibre. A representation of the center restricted to \\(A\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_n:=\\bar r_n-L_n(x_n)\n\\ge2W_n+\\frac12\\overline{\n\\frac{\\|P_Ez_n^s\\|_2^2}{\\|z_n^s\\|_2}}\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith a zero quotient interpreted as zero. Here\n\\[\n\\|z\\|_2-\\|P_Az\\|_2\\ge\\frac{\\|P_Ez\\|_2^2}{2\\|z\\|_2}.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(Q=\\sum_n\\bar r_n\\Delta_n\\). Since \\(\\|z_n^s\\|_2\\le2\\bar r_n\\),\n\\[\n\\|P_E\\bar z\\|_2^2\\le\\overline{\\|P_Ez^s\\|_2^2}\\le4Q,\n\\qquad \\sum_n\\Delta_n^2\\le Q,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ\\le\\sum_n\\bar r_n^2-\\|x\\|^2\n\\le(1+\\lambda)^2\\overline{\\|x^s\\|^2}-\\|x\\|^2.\n\\]</p><p>Take a norming functional for \\(P_Ey\\), precomposed with \\(P_E=P_{A'}-P_A\\). Its array \\(\\phi\\), supported on \\(E\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\langle y,\\phi\\rangle=\\|P_Ey\\|,\\qquad \\|\\phi\\|_2\\le2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_nc_n^2\\right)^{1/2}\\le2,\n\\qquad c_n=\\sup_{t\\in T_n}\\left|\\sum_{v\\preceq t}\\phi(v)\\right|.\n\\]\nThe second bound follows by testing one root path per component with arbitrary Euclidean scalar weights. The Hilbert contribution to\n\\(\\langle y,\\phi\\rangle=\\overline{s\\langle x^s,\\phi\\rangle}\\)\nis at most \\(4\\sqrt Q\\).</p><p>For the path part put \\(g(t)=\\sum_{v\\preceq t}\\phi(v)\\) and\n\\[\nI_n=\\int s\\,h(s,t)g(t)\\,dm_n(s,t).\n\\]\nFor \\(v\\in E\\), let \\(m(v)\\) be total descendant pair mass and \\(w(v)\\) its wrong-sign part. The descendant set is in one fibre, and \\(x(v)=0\\) gives\n\\[\n(B_n\\bar\\alpha_n)(v)\n=l_{\\pi_A(v)}(m(v)-2w(v))=-\\bar z(v).\n\\]\nWhen \\(w(v)\\le m(v)/4\\), therefore \\(m(v)\\le2|\\bar z(v)|\\).</p><p>Retain vertices for which this condition holds at every preceding vertex in the window. First failures form an antichain, with disjoint descendant mass at most \\(4W_n\\). The remainder of \\(g(t)\\) is a difference of two prefix sums, of magnitude at most \\(2c_n\\), contributing at most \\(8c_nW_n\\). The retained contribution is at most\n\\(2\\sum_{v\\in E\\cap T_n}|\\phi(v)||\\bar z(v)|\\).\nThus\n\\[\n\\sum_n|I_n|\n\\le4\\|P_E\\bar z\\|_2+4\\sum_nc_n\\Delta_n\n\\le16\\sqrt Q.\n\\]\nTogether with the Hilbert part, \\(\\|P_Ey\\|\\le20\\sqrt Q\\). Square and let \\(\\lambda\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 10", "orig": "Theorem 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions and assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions and assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(\\Omega=\\{-1,1\\}^m\\) with uniform probability, let \\(\\mathcal F_i\\) be generated by the first \\(i\\) signs and let \\(\\mathbb E_i\\) denote conditional expectation.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x_i=\\mathbb E_ix_m\\), \\(0\\le i\\le m\\), be an \\(X\\)-valued dyadic martingale.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_0\\) be a fixed finite initial set. For \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), let \\(P_i\\) be a finite initial set measurable with respect to \\(\\mathcal F_{i-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On every history suppose\n  \\[\n  P_{i-1}\\subseteq P_i,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{supp}x_i\\subseteq P_i,\\qquad \\|x_m\\|\\le C.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Delta_i=P_i\\setminus P_{i-1}\\), and write \\(R_D\\) for coordinate restriction to \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E\\|R_{\\Delta_i}x_i\\|^2\\le495C^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Only finitely many coordinates occur over the finite set of histories. By the finite-head convex-hull description in Proposition 7, on a finite extension of the probability space choose, componentwise, one random Hilbert atom or signed root path:\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[h_n+c_nb_{u_n}\\mid\\mathcal F_m]=x_m^n,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_n(\\|h_n\\|_2^2+|c_n|^2)\\le C^2.\n\\]\nIn each component either \\(c_n=0\\), or \\(h_n=0\\) and\n\\(|c_n|=L_n(x_m^n)\\). The filtration still records only the original signs.</p><p>Let \\(v_i^n\\) be the deepest vertex of the path to \\(u_n\\) in \\(P_i\\), or \\(\\partial\\) if absent, with \\(b_\\partial=0\\). Then\n\\[\nI_i^n=R_{\\Delta_i\\cap T_n}b_{u_n}\n=b_{v_i^n}-b_{v_{i-1}^n},\\qquad L_n(I_i^n)\\le2.\n\\]\nThe sigma-fields \\(\\mathcal G_i^n=\\sigma(\\mathcal F_i,v_i^n)\\) increase, since truncation recovers the previous trace. Set\n\\(a_i^n=\\mathbb E[c_n\\mid\\mathcal G_i^n]\\). Scalar orthogonality gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{n,i}\\mathbb E|a_i^n-a_{i-1}^n|^2\n\\le\\sum_n\\mathbb E|c_n|^2\\le C^2.\n\\]</p><p>Split\n\\[\nR_{\\Delta_i}x_i=H_i+M_i+W_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nH_i=\\mathbb E_iR_{\\Delta_i}(h_n)_n,\\quad\nM_i=\\mathbb E_i(a_{i-1}^nI_i^n)_n,\\quad\nW_i=\\mathbb E_i((a_i^n-a_{i-1}^n)I_i^n)_n.\n\\]\nThe identity uses \\(\\mathcal G_i^n\\)-measurability of \\(I_i^n\\).\nConditional Jensen and disjoint windows give\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E\\|H_i\\|^2\\le C^2,\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E\\|W_i\\|^2\\le4C^2.\n\\]\nUse primes for \\(\\mathbb E_{i-1}\\). Predictability and support of \\(x_{i-1}\\) imply \\(H_i'+M_i'+W_i'=0\\), hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E\\|M_i'\\|^2\\le10C^2.\n\\]</p><p>To compare a child and parent mixture, fix a component and parent history. For \\(v\\in\\Delta_i\\), let \\(r(v)\\) be its first window ancestor. On outcomes with trace \\(v_i^n=v\\), the old coefficient is a scalar \\(q_{r(v)}\\), determined by the old trace cell. Put it zero on null cells. Let \\(\\mu(v)\\) and \\(\\widetilde\\mu(v)\\) be the parent and specified-child trace probabilities; fairness gives \\(\\widetilde\\mu(v)\\le2\\mu(v)\\). With\n\\(I(v)=b_v-b_{r(v)^-}\\), interpreting missing predecessors as zero,\n\\[\nM_i^n=\\sum_vq_{r(v)}\\widetilde\\mu(v)I(v),\n\\qquad\n(M_i')^n=\\sum_vq_{r(v)}\\mu(v)I(v).\n\\]\nFor an admissible polar test \\(a\\), put\n\\(A_v=\\sum_{w\\in[r(v),v]}a_w\\). Define, zero off the window,\n\\[\nf_v=\\operatorname{sign}(q_{r(v)})(|A_v|-|A_{v^-}|),\n\\]\nwith predecessor value zero at first window vertices.\nThen \\(\\|f\\|_2\\le\\|a\\|_2\\), and each prefix sum telescopes to a signed \\(|A_v|\\le2\\). Thus \\(f/2\\) is admissible, and\n\\[\n|\\langle M_i^n,a\\rangle|\n\\le2\\sum_v|q_{r(v)}|\\mu(v)|A_v|\n=2\\langle(M_i')^n,f\\rangle.\n\\]\nHence \\(L_n(M_i^n)\\le4L_n((M_i')^n)\\), and \\(\\|M_i\\|\\le4\\|M_i'\\|\\). Its energy is at most \\(160C^2\\). Finally,\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E\\|H_i+M_i+W_i\\|^2\n\\le3(1+160+4)C^2=495C^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 11", "orig": "Theorem 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under all the assumptions and definitions of Theorem 10, \\( \\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E\\|R_{\\Delta_i}x_i\\|^2\\le252C^2. \\) In particular, the bound is \\(1008\\) when \\(\\|x_m\\|\\le2\\).", "statement": "<p>Under all the assumptions and definitions of Theorem 10,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E\\|R_{\\Delta_i}x_i\\|^2\\le252C^2.\n\\]\nIn particular, the bound is \\(1008\\) when \\(\\|x_m\\|\\le2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every terminal history choose component representations\n\\[\nx_m^n=h_n+B_nc_n,\\qquad\n\\|h_n\\|_2+\\|c_n\\|_1\\le2L_n(x_m^n),\n\\]\nzero in zero components. Put\n\\[\nh_i=\\mathbb E_i(h_n)_n,\\qquad\nb_i=(B_n\\mathbb E_ic_n)_n.\n\\]\nThen \\(x_i=h_i+b_i\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_n\\|h_n\\|_2^2\\le4C^2,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum_n\\|c_n\\|_1^2\\le4C^2.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 11 on each parent history with\n\\(S=P_{i-1}\\cap T_n\\), \\(T=P_i\\cap T_n\\), and the two child values of \\(b_i^n\\). If \\(g_{i,n}\\) is its gain, then\n\\[\nV_{P_{i-1}\\cap T_n}(b_{i-1}^n)^2+g_{i,n}^2\n\\le\\mathbb E_{i-1}V_{P_i\\cap T_n}(b_i^n)^2.\n\\]\nIndeed the average variation equals the old variation plus the nonnegative gain; square and use scalar Jensen. Telescoping and\n\\(V_R(B_nc)\\le\\|c\\|_1\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,n}\\mathbb Eg_{i,n}^2\\le4C^2.\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(R_{\\Delta_i}x_{i-1}=0\\), Proposition 11 gives\n\\[\n\\|R_{\\Delta_i}x_i\\|\n\\le\\|R_{\\Delta_i}h_i\\|_2+\n2\\|R_{\\Delta_i}h_{i-1}\\|_2+\n4\\left(\\sum_ng_{i,n}^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nPredictability gives\n\\(R_{\\Delta_i}h_j=\\mathbb E_jR_{\\Delta_i}(h_n)_n\\)\nfor \\(j=i-1,i\\). Jensen and pathwise disjointness bound either unscaled Hilbert energy by \\(4C^2\\). Squaring the three terms and summing gives\n\\[\n3(1+4+16)4C^2=252C^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 12", "orig": "Theorem 6.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3 and the dyadic filtration on \\(\\{-1,1\\}^m\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3 and the dyadic filtration on \\(\\{-1,1\\}^m\\). Let \\(x_t=\\mathbb E_tx_m\\), \\(0\\le t\\le m\\).</p><p>For \\(0\\le t&lt;m\\), let \\(A_t\\subseteq D_t\\) be finite initial sets measurable with respect to \\(\\mathcal F_t\\), and suppose\n\\[\nD_t\\subseteq A_{t+1}\\qquad(t+1&lt;m).\n\\]\nPut \\(U_t=D_t\\setminus A_t\\). No support condition on \\(x_t\\) is imposed.</p><p>There is an absolute constant \\(K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}\\mathbb E\\|R_{U_t}x_{t+1}\\|^2\n\\le K\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\|x_m\\|^2+\n\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}\\mathbb E\\|R_{U_t}x_t\\|^2\n\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose terminal representations\n\\[\nx_m^n=h_n+B_n\\lambda_n,\\qquad\n\\|h_n\\|_2+\\|\\lambda_n\\|_1\\le2L_n(x_m^n).\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\lambda_n=\\epsilon_n\\mu_n\\), where \\(\\mu_n=|\\lambda_n|\\), \\(|\\epsilon_n|\\le1\\), and \\(M_n=\\mu_n(T_n)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_n(\\|h_n\\|_2^2+M_n^2)\\le4\\mathbb E\\|x_m\\|^2.\n\\]\nOn outcome–endpoint pairs use the positive measure\n\\[\n\\nu_n(\\{\\omega\\}\\times\\{u\\})=2^{-m}\\mu_n(\\omega,\\{u\\}).\n\\]\nConditional sign means below use \\(\\nu_n\\), not the original fair sign law.</p><p>Let \\(a_{t,n}^-\\) be the conditional mean of \\(\\epsilon_n\\) given the first \\(t\\) signs and path truncation to \\(A_t\\). Let \\(a_{t,n}^+\\) use the first \\(t+1\\) signs and truncation to \\(D_t\\). Empty traces use a dummy symbol; null cells have mean zero. The partitions ordered\n\\((0,-),(0,+),(1,-),(1,+),\\ldots\\) refine successively. Put\n\\[\nq_{t,n}=\\mathbb E_{t+1}\\int|a_{t,n}^+-a_{t,n}^-|\\,d\\mu_n.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a component temporarily. Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\nq_t^2\\le(\\mathbb E_{t+1}M)\n\\mathbb E_{t+1}\\int|a_t^+-a_t^-|^2\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nFairness and \\(M\\ge0\\) give \\(\\mathbb E_{t+1}M\\le2\\mathbb E_tM\\).\nPut \\(R_t=\\max_{0\\le j\\le t}\\mathbb E_jM\\), \\(R_{-1}=0\\).\nConversion to the pair measure bounds the summed expectations by\n\\[\n2\\sum_{t&lt;m}\\int R_t|a_t^+-a_t^-|^2\\,d\\nu.\n\\]\nExpand \\(R_t=\\sum_{j\\le t}(R_j-R_{j-1})\\). Each increment is constant on cells \\(C\\) defining \\(a_j^-\\). Orthogonality on the refining pair partitions gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=j}^{m-1}\\int_C|a_t^+-a_t^-|^2\\,d\\nu\n\\le\\int_C|\\epsilon-a_j^-|^2\\,d\\nu\\le\\nu(C).\n\\]\nConsequently the weighted sum is at most\n\\(2\\int R_{m-1}\\,d\\nu=2\\mathbb E(MR_{m-1})\\).</p><p>For the scalar maximal estimate, let \\(R=\\max_{j\\le m}\\mathbb E_jM\\).\nPartition \\(\\{R\\ge u\\}\\) by first crossings to obtain\n\\[\nu\\mathbb P(R\\ge u)\\le\\mathbb E[M1_{\\{R\\ge u\\}}].\n\\]\nIntegrating yields \\(\\mathbb ER^2\\le2\\mathbb E(MR)\\), so\n\\(\\|R\\|_2\\le2\\|M\\|_2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_{t&lt;m}\\mathbb Eq_{t,n}^2\\le4\\mathbb EM_n^2.\n\\]</p><p>For a vertex \\(s\\), let \\(C_s=\\{u:s\\preceq u\\}\\). Define\n\\[\n\\lambda_{t+1}'=\\mathbb E_{t+1}(a_t^-\\mu_n),\\qquad\n\\rho_{t+1}=\\mathbb E_{t+1}((a_t^+-a_t^-)\\mu_n).\n\\]\nOn \\(s\\in U_t\\), measurability of the cone in the \\(a_t^+\\)-partition gives the correct child path coordinates from \\(\\lambda_{t+1}'+\\rho_{t+1}\\), with\n\\(\\|\\rho_{t+1}\\|_1\\le q_{t,n}\\).\nLet \\(h_{t,n}=\\mathbb E_th_n\\), and average these measures to time \\(t\\). Then\n\\[\nx_t^n(s)=h_{t,n}(s)+\\lambda_t'(C_s)+\\rho_t(C_s).\n\\]\nAt a fixed parent history, \\(a_t^-\\) is constant on each window cone, so there is no cancellation there. If\n\\(\\phi_s=|\\lambda_t'(C_s)|\\), fairness gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda_{t+1}'|(C_s)\\le2\\phi_s.\n\\]</p><p>Decompose the parent window:\n\\[\nR_{U_t}x_t^n=d+B_n\\theta,\\qquad\n\\|d\\|_2+\\|\\theta\\|_1\\le2r_{t,n},\n\\quad r_{t,n}=L_n(R_{U_t}x_t^n).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\beta=|\\theta|+|\\rho_t|\\),\n\\[\n\\phi_s\\le|d(s)-h_{t,n}(s)|+\\beta(C_s),\n\\qquad\n\\|\\beta\\|_1\\le\\|\\theta\\|_1+\\mathbb E_tq_{t,n}.\n\\]\nStop at the first window vertices with \\(\\beta(C_s)\\ge\\phi_s/2\\).\nTheir cones are disjoint. Above the stops the child path coordinates have Hilbert norm at most\n\\(4(\\|d\\|_2+\\|R_{U_t}h_{t,n}\\|_2)\\).\nIn stopping cones the child path mass is at most \\(4\\|\\beta\\|_1\\), and its window paths cost at most two. The error measure costs at most \\(2q_{t,n}\\). Hence\n\\[\nL_n(R_{U_t}x_{t+1}^n)\n\\le\\|R_{U_t}h_{t+1,n}\\|_2+\n4\\|R_{U_t}h_{t,n}\\|_2+\n16r_{t,n}+8\\mathbb E_tq_{t,n}+2q_{t,n}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(j=t,t+1\\), Jensen and predictability bound\n\\(\\mathbb E\\|R_{U_t}h_{j,n}\\|_2^2\\) by\n\\(\\mathbb E\\|R_{U_t}h_n\\|_2^2\\).\nThe windows are pathwise disjoint. The sign-error energies are bounded by the estimate already proved and Jensen. Square the five-term bound using five times the sum of squares, sum, and use the terminal budget. This proves the assertion with an absolute constant. Tonelli justifies all component sums, including infinitely many nonzero components.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 13", "orig": "Theorem 6.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(W_i=\\mathbb E_iW_m\\), \\(0\\le i\\le m\\), be a dyadic martingale.", "statement": "<p>In the linear path space \\(X\\) of Theorem 3, let \\(W_i=\\mathbb E_iW_m\\), \\(0\\le i\\le m\\), be a dyadic martingale. Let\n\\[\nA_0\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq A_m\n\\]\nbe fixed finite initial sets, and put \\(\\Delta_i=A_i\\setminus A_{i-1}\\).</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E\\|R_{\\Delta_i}W_i\\|^2\n\\le1024\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\|W_m\\|^2+\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E\\|R_{\\Delta_i}W_{i-1}\\|^2\n\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work first in one component. Choose terminal representations\n\\[\nW_m=H+B_nc,\\qquad\n\\|H\\|_2+\\|c\\|_1\\le2L_n(W_m),\n\\]\nand set \\(H_i=\\mathbb E_iH\\). Let \\(\\pi_i(s)\\) be the deepest ancestor of \\(s\\) in \\(A_i\\), or \\(\\partial\\); put \\(b_\\partial=0\\). Define\n\\[\nc_r^i=\\mathbb E_i\\sum_{\\pi_i(s)=r}c_s,\\qquad\nv_i=\\sum_{r\\in A_i\\cup\\{\\partial\\}}|c_r^i|.\n\\]\nKeeping the dummy coefficient gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{i-1}\\sum_{\\pi_{i-1}(r)=q}c_r^i=c_q^{i-1}.\n\\]\nThe child window is\n\\[\nR_{\\Delta_i}W_i=R_{\\Delta_i}H_i+\\sum_rc_r^iR_{\\Delta_i}b_r.\n\\]</p><p>Orient by\n\\[\n\\sigma_r=\\operatorname{sign}(c_{\\pi_{i-1}(r)}^{i-1}),\n\\qquad c_r^i=\\sigma_r(g_r-q_r),\n\\qquad g_r,q_r\\ge0,\n\\]\nchoosing sign \\(+1\\) at zero. Then\n\\[\n\\beta_i:=\\mathbb E_{i-1}\\sum_rq_r\n=\\frac{\\mathbb E_{i-1}v_i-v_{i-1}}2\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe square inequality telescopes:\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E\\beta_i^2\\le\\frac14\\mathbb Ev_m^2\n\\le\\frac14\\mathbb E\\|c\\|_1^2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(t\\in\\Delta_i\\), all descendant labels have the same old sign. Put\n\\(g(t)=\\sum_{r\\succeq t}g_r\\), \\(q(t)=\\sum_{r\\succeq t}q_r\\).\nDecompose the leaked parent window\n\\[\nY=R_{\\Delta_i}W_{i-1}=L+B_nd,\\qquad\n\\|L\\|_2+\\|d\\|_1\\le2L_n(Y).\n\\]\nFair binary averaging gives\n\\[\ng(t)\\le2\\left(\n|L(t)|+|H_{i-1}(t)|+\n\\sum_{s\\succeq t}|d_s|+\\mathbb E_{i-1}q(t)\n\\right).\n\\]\nCall \\(t\\) large when\n\\(g(t)&gt;4(|L(t)|+|H_{i-1}(t)|)\\). At such nodes,\n\\[\ng(t)\\le4\\left(\\sum_{s\\succeq t}|d_s|\n+\\mathbb E_{i-1}q(t)\\right).\n\\]\nMinimal large nodes form an antichain. Mark all descendant coefficient labels. Their positive mass is at most \\(4(\\|d\\|_1+\\beta_i)\\); total negative mass is at most \\(2\\beta_i\\). Their window path cost is at most\n\\(8\\|d\\|_1+12\\beta_i\\).\nThe unmarked positive occupancy vanishes at large nodes and elsewhere is bounded by \\(4(|L|+|H_{i-1}|)\\). Thus\n\\[\nL_n(R_{\\Delta_i}W_i)\n\\le\\|R_{\\Delta_i}H_i\\|_2+\n4\\|R_{\\Delta_i}H_{i-1}\\|_2+\n12\\beta_i+16L_n(R_{\\Delta_i}W_{i-1}).\n\\]\nFor \\(j=i-1,i\\), Jensen and fixed disjoint windows bound the Hilbert energy by \\(\\mathbb E\\|H\\|_2^2\\). Squaring four terms and using the \\(\\beta_i\\)-estimate gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mathbb EL_n(R_{\\Delta_i}W_i)^2\n\\le68\\mathbb E\\|H\\|_2^2+\n144\\mathbb E\\|c\\|_1^2+\n1024\\sum_i\\mathbb EL_n(R_{\\Delta_i}W_{i-1})^2.\n\\]\nThe first two terms are at most \\(576\\mathbb EL_n(W_m)^2\\).\nSum over components, and replace \\(576\\) by \\(1024\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 14", "orig": "Proposition 7.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\ge1\\), let \\(T_n=\\mathbb N^{\\le n}\\), \\(H_n=\\ell_2(T_n)\\), and\n  \\[\n  p_t=\\sum_{s\\preceq t}e_s,\\qquad\n  J_n\\lambda=\\sum_{t\\in T_n}\\lambda_tp_t.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(Z_n=(H_n,|\\cdot|_n)\\), where\n  \\[\n  |z|_n=\\inf_{z=h+J_n\\lambda}\n       \\bigl(\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\lambda\\|_1^2\\bigr)^{1/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  X_q=\\left(\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}Z_n\\right)_{\\ell_2},\n  \\qquad\n  \\|z\\|_q^2=\\sum_n|z_n|_n^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), let \\(P_A\\) retain its coordinates and \\(Q_A=I-P_A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(X_q\\) is reflexive and admits no equivalent AUC norm. More precisely, if\n\\[\na\\|z\\|_q\\le N(z)\\le b\\|z\\|_q,\\qquad0&lt;a\\le b&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\overline\\delta_N\\!\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt2\\,b}\\right)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 12 proves reflexivity and gives\n\\[\n1/\\sqrt2\\le\\|p_s\\|_q,\\|e_s\\|_q\\le1.\n\\]\nFor a fixed nonterminal \\(s\\in T_n\\), the children \\(e_{s^\\frown j}\\) are weakly null in the Hilbert-equivalent block and hence in \\(X_q\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\overline\\delta_N(t_0)&gt;0\\), where \\(t_0=a/(\\sqrt2\\,b)\\). Choose \\(c&gt;0\\) with \\(2c&lt;\\overline\\delta_N(t_0)\\). At\n\\(u=p_s/N(p_s)\\), choose finite-codimensional \\(F\\) such that\n\\[\nN(u+t_0v)&gt;1+2c\\qquad(v\\in F,\\ N(v)=1).\n\\]\nThe normalized children\n\\[\nv_j=e_{s^\\frown j}/N(e_{s^\\frown j})\n\\]\nare weakly null, their denominators being bounded below. Approximation in \\(F\\), followed by normalization, gives\n\\(N(u+t_0v_j)&gt;1+c\\) for large \\(j\\).</p><p>Convexity preserves this gain at radii at least \\(t_0\\). Since\n\\[\nN(e_{s^\\frown j})/N(p_s)\\ge a/(\\sqrt2\\,b),\n\\]\none can select\n\\[\nN(p_{s^\\frown j})\\ge(1+c)N(p_s).\n\\]\nThrough height \\(n\\), this gives \\(b\\ge(1+c)^na/\\sqrt2\\), impossible for arbitrarily large \\(n\\). Nonnegativity of the modulus gives zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 15", "orig": "Theorem 7.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the quadratic path space \\(X_q\\) of Theorem 14, let \\(A\\) be a finite ancestral head and \\(P_Ax=x\\).", "statement": "<p>In the quadratic path space \\(X_q\\) of Theorem 14, let \\(A\\) be a finite ancestral head and \\(P_Ax=x\\). For every \\(y\\in X_q\\) and \\(R\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\|x+y\\|_q,\\ \\|x-y\\|_q\\le R\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le8\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|_q^2}.\n\\]\nThe displacement \\(y\\) need not vanish on the head.</p>", "proof": "<p>Convexity gives \\(\\|x\\|_q\\le R\\); the case \\(R=0\\) is immediate. Choose the representations of Proposition 13 with each squared cost at most \\(R^2+\\eta\\). Write\n\\[\nD=R^2+\\eta-\\|x\\|_q^2,\n\\]\nso the deficit \\(E\\le D\\).</p><p>In each component choose the majority sign \\(\\epsilon_r\\) of both labeled endpoint arrays in every folding fibre. If their positive and negative full masses are \\(a_r,b_r\\), the nondiscarded contribution to \\(d_n\\) is \\(\\min(a_r,b_r)\\); the discarded contribution is \\((a_r+b_r)/2\\), at least their minimum. Thus total minority mass is at most \\(d_n\\).</p><p>Outside the head define\n\\[\nU^\\epsilon(t)=\\sum_{s\\succeq t}(\\epsilon_{r(s)}\\lambda_n^\\epsilon(s))_+,\n\\quad\nV^\\epsilon(t)=\\sum_{s\\succeq t}(-\\epsilon_{r(s)}\\lambda_n^\\epsilon(s))_+,\n\\quad V=V^++V^-.\n\\]\nThese are stopped flows. Their common orientation is constant on each outside subtree, and\n\\[\n\\epsilon_{r(t)}(U^+(t)+U^-(t)-V(t))\n=-h_n^+(t)-h_n^-(t).\n\\]\nLet \\(S=\\min(U^+,V)\\). It has initial mass at most \\(d_n\\), and\n\\[\n0\\le U^+-S\n\\le(U^++U^--V)_+\\le|h_n^++h_n^-|.\n\\]\nThe tail of \\(y_n\\) is the sum of signed flows \\(S,-V^+\\) and the Hilbert vector \\(h_n^++\\epsilon_r(U^+-S)\\). Proposition 14 gives\n\\[\n|Q_Ay_n|_n\n\\le4d_n+\\|Q_Ah_n^+\\|_2+\n\\|Q_A(h_n^++h_n^-)\\|_2.\n\\]\nTake the outer Euclidean norm. Proposition 13 bounds these three terms by\n\\(4\\sqrt D,\\sqrt{2D},2\\sqrt D\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le(6+\\sqrt2)\\sqrt D\\le8\\sqrt D.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\); no minimizing representation is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 16", "orig": "Corollary 7.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_q\\), suppose an infinite sequence \\((y_j)\\) satisfies \\( \\|x\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le R,\\qquad \\|y_i-y_j\\|_q\\ge\\varepsilon>0\\quad(i\\ne j). \\) Then \\( \\|x\\|_q^2+\\frac{\\varepsilon^2}{256}\\le R^2. \\)", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_q\\), suppose an infinite sequence \\((y_j)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|x\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le R,\\qquad\n\\|y_i-y_j\\|_q\\ge\\varepsilon&gt;0\\quad(i\\ne j).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_q^2+\\frac{\\varepsilon^2}{256}\\le R^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Approximate \\(x\\) within \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) by \\(x_0\\) supported in a finite ancestral head \\(A\\). Theorem 15 bounds each \\(Q_Ay_j\\) by\n\\[\n8\\sqrt{(R+\\rho)^2-\\|x_0\\|_q^2}.\n\\]\nThe sequence is bounded and \\(P_A\\) has finite rank, so two distinct head projections can be arbitrarily close. Their difference and the two tail bounds give\n\\[\n\\varepsilon\\le16\\sqrt{(R+\\rho)^2-\\|x_0\\|_q^2}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\rho\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 17", "orig": "Proposition 7.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_q\\), for a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), if \\(P_Ax=x\\) and \\(P_Ay=0\\), then \\( \\|x\\|_q^2+\\frac{\\|y\\|_q^2}{144} \\le\\frac{\\|x+y\\|_q^2+\\|x-y\\|_q^2}{2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_q\\), for a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), if \\(P_Ax=x\\) and \\(P_Ay=0\\), then\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_q^2+\\frac{\\|y\\|_q^2}{144}\n\\le\\frac{\\|x+y\\|_q^2+\\|x-y\\|_q^2}{2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in one component and choose representations whose squared costs exceed \\(|x\\pm y|_n^2\\) by at most \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). Use Proposition 13, with\n\\[\nE=\\frac{|x+y|_n^2+|x-y|_n^2}{2}+\\eta-|x|_n^2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\|Q\\bar h\\|_2^2+\\|\\widetilde h\\|_2^2+M^2-q^2\\le E.\n\\]</p><p>Orient nondiscarded fibres by the sign of their folded mean, choosing \\(+1\\) at zero and either sign for discarded fibres. Define half-weighted masses\n\\[\n\\alpha_t^\\epsilon=\\tfrac12(\\epsilon_{r(t)}\\lambda_t^\\epsilon)_+,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_t^\\epsilon=\\tfrac12(-\\epsilon_{r(t)}\\lambda_t^\\epsilon)_+.\n\\]\nWith \\(\\beta=\\beta^++\\beta^-\\), one has\n\\(\\|\\beta\\|_1\\le M-q\\le\\sqrt E\\).</p><p>Outside the head let \\(A^\\epsilon,B^\\epsilon\\) be their descendant occupancies, and put\n\\(A_{\\rm tot}=A^++A^-\\), \\(B_{\\rm tot}=B^++B^-\\). Then\n\\[\n0=\\bar h+\\epsilon_r(A_{\\rm tot}-B_{\\rm tot}),\n\\]\n\\[\ny=\\widetilde h+\\epsilon_r(A^+-A^--B^++B^-).\n\\]\nFor each sign let\n\\(S^\\epsilon=\\min(A^\\epsilon,B_{\\rm tot})\\),\n\\(V^\\epsilon=A^\\epsilon-S^\\epsilon\\).\nThen\n\\[\n0\\le V^\\epsilon\\le(A_{\\rm tot}-B_{\\rm tot})_+\\le|\\bar h|.\n\\]\nThe four flows \\(S^\\pm,B^\\pm\\) each have initial mass at most \\(\\|\\beta\\|_1\\), so Proposition 14 gives total norm at most \\(8\\|\\beta\\|_1\\). The other terms have Hilbert norm at most\n\\(\\|Q\\widetilde h\\|_2+2\\|Q\\bar h\\|_2\\). Since \\(y\\) vanishes on the head,\n\\[\n|y|_n\\le\\|Q\\widetilde h\\|_2+\n2\\|Q\\bar h\\|_2+8\\|\\beta\\|_1\\le12\\sqrt E.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\), square, and sum over components. Empty heads are handled by their discarded fibre.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 18", "orig": "Corollary 7.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_q\\), suppose \\( \\|x\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le r,\\qquad \\|y_i-y_j\\|_q\\ge2d>0\\quad(i\\ne j) \\) for an infinite sequence.", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_q\\), suppose\n\\[\n\\|x\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le r,\\qquad\n\\|y_i-y_j\\|_q\\ge2d&gt;0\\quad(i\\ne j)\n\\]\nfor an infinite sequence. Then\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_q^2+d^2/144\\le r^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finite-support \\(x_0\\) within \\(\\epsilon\\) of \\(x\\), and an ancestral head \\(A\\) supporting it. Boundedness and finite rank give distinct \\(i,j\\) with\n\\[\n\\|P_Az\\|_q&lt;\\epsilon,\\qquad z=(y_i-y_j)/2.\n\\]\nConvexity gives \\(\\|x\\pm z\\|_q\\le r\\), and separation gives \\(\\|z\\|_q\\ge d\\). The vectors \\(x_0,Q_Az\\) have endpoint norms at most \\(r+2\\epsilon\\), and\n\\(\\|Q_Az\\|_q\\ge d-\\epsilon\\). Apply Theorem 17 and let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 19", "orig": "Proposition 7.10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Theorem 15, \\( \\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le7\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|_q^2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Theorem 15,\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le7\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|_q^2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose endpoint representations with squared costs at most \\((R+\\eta)^2\\), and use Proposition 13. Put\n\\[\nm_n=(|\\lambda_n^+|+|\\lambda_n^-|)/2,\n\\qquad\nE=(R+\\eta)^2-\\|x\\|_q^2.\n\\]\nThen \\(|\\sigma_n|,|\\tau_n|\\le m_n\\), and\n\\[\n\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\|\\widetilde h\\|_H^2+\\sum_nd_n^2\\le E.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(S_t=\\{s:s\\succeq t\\}\\). Mark an outside node when\n\\[\n|\\sigma_n(S_t)|&lt;m_n(S_t)/2,\n\\]\nand keep only first marked nodes. Their descendant sets are disjoint. For disjoint subsets \\(S_j\\) of a nondiscarded fibre \\(C\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_j(m_n(S_j)-|\\sigma_n(S_j)|)\n\\le m_n(C)-|\\sigma_n(C)|.\n\\]\nThis follows by bounding \\(|\\sigma_n(C)|\\) by the displayed absolute sums plus the remaining \\(m_n\\)-mass, also for countably many subsets. Discarded fibres are bounded by their whole mass. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\text{ first marked}}m_n(S_t)\\le2d_n.\n\\]</p><p>Split \\(\\tau_n=\\tau_n^r+\\tau_n^k\\), with the removed part on these descendant sets. Then\n\\[\n\\|\\tau_n^r\\|_1\\le2d_n,\\qquad\n\\|QJ\\tau^r\\|_q\\le4\\left(\\sum_nd_n^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nAt a node with a marked ancestor the kept occupancy vanishes. Otherwise\n\\[\n|(J_n\\tau_n^k)(t)|\n\\le m_n(S_t)\\le2|\\sigma_n(S_t)|=2|\\bar h_n(t)|,\n\\]\nusing \\(Qx=0\\). Hence the kept tail is Hilbert, with norm at most \\(2\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H\\). Since \\(y=\\widetilde h+J\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\|Qy\\|_q\\le\\|Q\\widetilde h\\|_H+\n2\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H+\n4\\left(\\sum_nd_n^2\\right)^{1/2}\\le7\\sqrt E.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 20", "orig": "Corollary 7.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_q\\), let \\(0<c\\le1\\), and put \\(\\delta=c^2/2000\\).", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_q\\), let \\(0&lt;c\\le1\\), and put \\(\\delta=c^2/2000\\). If an infinite sequence satisfies\n\\[\n\\|x\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le1,\\qquad\n\\|y_i-y_j\\|_q\\ge2c\\quad(i\\ne j),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_q\\le1-\\delta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(\\|x\\|_q&gt;1-\\delta\\). Choose finite-support \\(x_0\\) within \\(\\delta\\), and an ancestral head \\(A\\) supporting it. Then\n\\[\n\\|x_0\\|_q&gt;1-2\\delta,\\qquad\n\\|x_0\\pm y_j\\|_q\\le1+\\delta.\n\\]\nTheorem 19 gives\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ay_j\\|_q\n&lt;7\\sqrt{(1+\\delta)^2-(1-2\\delta)^2}\n\\le7\\sqrt{6\\delta}&lt;c/2.\n\\]\nTwo bounded head projections are less than \\(c\\) apart, contradicting the \\(2c\\)-separation of the full vectors.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 21", "orig": "Proposition 7.12", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Theorem 15, \\( \\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le9\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|_q^2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Theorem 15,\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ay\\|_q\\le9\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|_q^2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose representations with squared costs at most \\(R^2+\\eta\\), and put\n\\(E=R^2+\\eta-\\|x\\|_q^2\\). Orient each nondiscarded fibre by its folded coefficient, using \\(+1\\) at zero, and either sign on discarded fibres.</p><p>Let \\(g_n,b_n\\) be half the total agreeing and disagreeing absolute masses, counting both labeled endpoint arrays. Then \\(\\|b_n\\|_1\\le d_n\\). More precisely, if the head in this component is nonempty, \\(2\\|b_n\\|_1=d_n\\); if empty, \\(d_n=M_n\\).</p><p>Outside the head put \\(G(t)=g_n(S_t)\\), \\(B(t)=b_n(S_t)\\). Then\n\\[\n|J_n\\sigma_n(t)|=|G(t)-B(t)|=|\\bar h_n(t)|.\n\\]\nIn the labeled half-difference remove all minority terms and all majority terms whose endpoint has an outside ancestor with \\(G(t)\\le2B(t)\\). First such nodes have disjoint descendant sets. Removed majority mass is at most \\(2\\|b_n\\|_1\\), so removed half-difference mass is at most \\(3d_n\\). This accounting is made before cancellation.</p><p>Kept occupancy vanishes below stopping nodes; elsewhere\n\\[\n|J_n\\tau_n^k(t)|\\le G(t)\\le2|G(t)-B(t)|=2|\\bar h_n(t)|.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n|Qy_n|_n\\le\\|Q\\widetilde h_n\\|_2+\n2\\|Q\\bar h_n\\|_2+6d_n.\n\\]\nProposition 13 and the outer Euclidean triangle inequality give\n\\(\\|Qy\\|_q\\le9\\sqrt E\\). Let \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 22", "orig": "Corollary 7.13", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\rho>0\\), choose \\(0<\\gamma<1/2\\) such that \\( 9\\sqrt{(1+\\gamma)^2-(1-2\\gamma)^2}<\\rho/3. \\) If \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(\\|x\\|_q\\ge1-\\gamma\\), then the symmetric lens …", "statement": "<p>For every \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), choose \\(0&lt;\\gamma&lt;1/2\\) such that\n\\[\n9\\sqrt{(1+\\gamma)^2-(1-2\\gamma)^2}&lt;\\rho/3.\n\\]\nIf \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(\\|x\\|_q\\ge1-\\gamma\\), then the symmetric lens\n\\[\n\\{y\\in X_q:\\|x+y\\|_q\\le1,\\ \\|x-y\\|_q\\le1\\}\n\\]\ncontains no infinite \\(\\rho\\)-separated subset.</p>", "proof": "<p>Approximate \\(x\\) within \\(\\gamma\\) by finite-support \\(x_0\\), and choose its ancestral head \\(A\\). Then \\(\\|x_0\\|_q&gt;1-2\\gamma\\), and every point \\(y\\) of the lens satisfies \\(\\|x_0\\pm y\\|_q\\le1+\\gamma\\). Theorem 21 gives\n\\(\\|Q_Ay\\|_q&lt;\\rho/3\\).\nLens points have norm at most one. In any infinite sequence two head projections are less than \\(\\rho/3\\) apart, so their full distance is less than \\(\\rho\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 23", "orig": "Proposition 7.14", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every nonzero \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(0<\\delta\\le1\\), there is a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), depending only on \\(x,\\delta\\), such that every \\(u\\in X_q\\) satisfies …", "statement": "<p>For every nonzero \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(0&lt;\\delta\\le1\\), there is a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), depending only on \\(x,\\delta\\), such that every \\(u\\in X_q\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|x+u\\|_q,\\ \\|x-u\\|_q\\le(1+\\delta)\\|x\\|_q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\|Q_Au\\|_q\\le72\\sqrt\\delta\\,\\|x\\|_q.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Scale to \\(\\|x\\|_q=1\\). Choose finite-support \\(x_0\\) within \\(\\delta\\), and an ancestral head \\(A\\) containing its support. This choice precedes \\(u\\). If the stated endpoint condition holds, then\n\\(\\|x_0\\pm u\\|_q\\le1+2\\delta\\).\nChoose representations with squared costs at most \\((1+3\\delta)^2\\).</p><p>Use the notation of Proposition 13 for these representations, and define\n\\[\nD=C-\\left(\\|P\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\sum_nq_n^2\\right).\n\\]\nBefore comparing the center representation with its norm, the same parallelogram calculation gives\n\\[\n\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\|\\widetilde h\\|_H^2+\n\\sum_n(M_n^2-q_n^2)\\le D,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|Qh^+\\|_H^2+\\|Qh^-\\|_H^2}{2}\n+\\sum_nd_n^2\\le D.\n\\]\nThe center representation has cost at least \\(\\|x_0\\|_q\\), so\n\\[\n0\\le D\\le(1+3\\delta)^2-(1-\\delta)^2\\le16\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>Orient fibres by their folded coefficients. Use full outside agreeing masses \\(p_n^\\pm\\) and disagreeing masses \\(b_n^\\pm\\), and put\n\\(B_n=\\|b_n^++b_n^-\\|_1\\). Then\n\\[\nB_n\\le2d_n,\\qquad\\sum_nB_n^2\\le4D.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nG(t)=(p_n^++p_n^-)(S_t),\\qquad\nV(t)=(b_n^++b_n^-)(S_t).\n\\]\nSince the averaged vector vanishes outside,\n\\[\n|G(t)-V(t)|=|h_n^+(t)+h_n^-(t)|.\n\\]\nStop at first outside nodes with \\(G(t)&gt;0\\) and \\(V(t)\\ge G(t)/2\\). Their disjoint cones contain majority mass at most \\(2B_n\\). Remove these majority terms and all minority terms from the outside part of \\(\\lambda_n^+\\). The removed array satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda_n^r\\|_1\\le3B_n,\\qquad\n\\|\\lambda^r\\|_L\\le6\\sqrt D,\\qquad\n\\|QJ\\lambda^r\\|_q\\le12\\sqrt D.\n\\]\nThe kept occupancy is everywhere bounded by \\(2|G-V|\\), so\n\\[\n\\|QJ\\lambda^k\\|_H\\le2\\|Q(h^++h^-)\\|_H\\le4\\sqrt D.\n\\]\nHead endpoints have zero tail, and \\(\\|Qh^+\\|_H\\le\\sqrt{2D}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\|Qu\\|_q\\le(12+4+\\sqrt2)\\sqrt D\n\\le18\\sqrt D\\le72\\sqrt\\delta.\n\\]\nFirst-ancestor stopping works unchanged with infinite support and countably many children. Rescale.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 24", "orig": "Corollary 7.17", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every unit vector \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(0<e\\le1/16\\), there is a closed finite-codimensional subspace \\(Y\\subseteq X_q\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>For every unit vector \\(x\\in X_q\\) and \\(0&lt;e\\le1/16\\), there is a closed finite-codimensional subspace \\(Y\\subseteq X_q\\) such that\n\\[\ny\\in Y,\\qquad \\|x+y\\|_q,\\ \\|x-y\\|_q\\le1+e\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\|y\\|_q\\le17(2e)^{1/6}.\n\\]\nIn particular, for every \\(t&gt;0\\), there is \\(e_t&gt;0\\), independent of the unit center \\(x\\), such that some closed finite-codimensional \\(Y\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\inf_{u\\in S_Y}\\max\\{\\|x+tu\\|_q,\\|x-tu\\|_q\\}\\ge1+e_t.\n\\]\nThus \\(X_q\\) has positive maximum asymptotic midpoint modulus at every positive radius.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a finitely supported unit vector \\(x_0\\) with \\(\\|x-x_0\\|_q&lt;e\\), and use the subspace \\(Y_{x_0}\\) of Proposition 15. Unit finite-support vectors are dense by approximation followed by normalization.</p><p>If \\(y\\in Y_{x_0}\\) and \\(\\|x\\pm y\\|_q\\le1+e\\), then\n\\(\\|x_0\\pm y\\|_q\\le1+2e\\).\nSince \\(2e\\le1/8\\), Proposition 15 gives\n\\(\\|y\\|_q\\le17(2e)^{1/6}\\).</p><p>For \\(t&gt;0\\), choose \\(0&lt;e_t\\le1/16\\) with\n\\(17(2e_t)^{1/6}&lt;t\\). No \\(tu\\), \\(u\\in S_Y\\), can have both endpoint norms at most \\(1+e_t\\). Thus each maximum exceeds \\(1+e_t\\), and its infimum is at least this value.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 25", "orig": "Theorem 8.4; the structural and renorming assertions preceding it are included", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">On disjoint copies of \\(T_n=\\mathbb N^{\\le n}\\), write\n  \\[\n  [t,v]=\\{s:t\\preceq s\\preceq v\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonnegative start-mass array \\(m\\), define upwards from the leaves\n  \\[\n  q_t(m)=m_t+\\left(\\sum_{s\\text{ child of }t}q_s(m)^2\\right)^{1/2},\n  \\qquad M_n(m)=q_{\\varnothing}(m),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  M(m)=\\left(\\sum_nM_n(m|_{T_n})^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A segment representation is an array \\(a=(a_{tv})_{t\\preceq v}\\), with\n  \\[\n  m_t(a)=\\sum_{v\\succeq t}|a_{tv}|,\n  \\qquad\n  (Pa)_s=\\sum_{t\\preceq s}\\sum_{v\\succeq s}a_{tv}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\|x\\|_{S,n}=\\inf_{Pa=x}M_n(m(a)),\n  \\qquad\n  X_S=\\left(\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}\n  (\\ell_2(T_n),\\|\\cdot\\|_{S,n})\\right)_{\\ell_2}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(X_S\\) is reflexive and admits no equivalent AUC norm. If \\(C\\) is a finite ancestral set, \\(p\\) is supported in \\(C\\), and \\(y|_C=0\\), then\n\\[\n\\max\\{\\|p+y\\|_S,\\|p-y\\|_S\\}^2\n\\ge\\|p\\|_S^2+\\frac{\\|y\\|_S^2}{64}.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(C=\\varnothing\\), \\(p=0\\), and \\(y=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 17 gives reflexivity, unit paths, and weakly null unit child increments. Proposition 2 therefore excludes every equivalent AUC norm, using \\(m=M=c=1\\). Explicitly, at scale \\(\\alpha/(2\\beta)\\), positive modulus would extend a path by a factor \\(1+\\overline\\delta_N(\\alpha/(2\\beta))/4\\) at each step, eventually exceeding its uniform bound.</p><p>For the midpoint inequality, fix \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and let\n\\[\nR=\\max\\{\\|p+y\\|_S,\\|p-y\\|_S\\}+\\eta.\n\\]\nChoose endpoint segment representations of costs at most \\(R\\). Halve all coefficients and concatenate their lists, keeping the endpoint-origin labels \\(+\\) and \\(-\\), which are independent of coefficient signs. The combined output is \\(p\\), and its start mass \\(m\\) satisfies \\(M(m)\\le R\\).</p><p>At each first node \\(s\\) outside \\(C\\), match positive and negative weights of segments containing \\(s\\), in pieces of equal absolute weight. The two total masses are finite and equal because the output at \\(s\\) is zero. A countable matching is obtained by placing the two lists consecutively on intervals of their common total length and taking interval intersections. Each segment meets at most one first outside node, so matchings do not interfere.</p><p>In each matched pair, both starts are ancestors of \\(s\\). Call the deeper start the later start, breaking ties arbitrarily. Let \\(r_t\\) be the total size of later pieces starting at \\(t\\), together with all pieces starting strictly below first outside nodes. These are disjoint portions of the list, so \\(0\\le r\\le m\\).</p><p>Recover \\(p\\) as follows. Delete pieces starting strictly below a first outside node. For a matched pair with coincident starts, discard both pieces. Otherwise discard the later piece and shorten the earlier one to end just before the later start. What remains lies in \\(C\\); removed overlapping pieces canceled there. The resulting start masses are at most \\(m-r\\). Proposition 16 gives\n\\[\n\\|p\\|_S^2+M(r)^2\n\\le M(m-r)^2+M(r)^2\\le M(m)^2.\n\\]</p><p>The exterior output of the \\(+\\)-list is \\(y/2\\). Reproduce it with start masses at most \\(4r\\). Keep its pieces starting strictly below first outside nodes. For a \\(+\\)-piece in a matched pair exiting at \\(s\\), let \\(t\\) be the pair's later start and \\(v\\) this piece's endpoint. Use \\([t,v]\\) with the piece's coefficient; if \\(t\\in C\\), subtract \\([t,s^-]\\) with the opposite coefficient. This leaves precisely its exterior part. If \\(t=s\\), subtraction is unnecessary. Each pair has at most two \\(+\\)-pieces, each using at most two segments, all charged at \\(t\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\|y\\|_S\\le2M(4r)=8M(r).\n\\]\nThese lists have finite start cost, and coordinate sums remain legitimate. Combining the estimates gives\n\\[\n\\|p\\|_S^2+\\|y\\|_S^2/64\\le R^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 26", "orig": "Corollary 8.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_S\\), for every unit \\(x\\), every \\(t>0\\), and every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a closed finite-codimensional subspace \\(F\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_S\\), for every unit \\(x\\), every \\(t&gt;0\\), and every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is a closed finite-codimensional subspace \\(F\\) such that\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\substack{y\\in F\\\\\\|y\\|_S=1}}\n\\max\\{\\|x+ty\\|_S,\\|x-ty\\|_S\\}\n\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/64}-\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nFor the maximum asymptotic midpoint modulus\n\\[\n\\delta_{X_S}^{\\max}(t)=\n\\inf_{\\|x\\|_S=1}\\sup_{F\\in\\operatorname{cof}(X_S)}\n\\inf_{\\substack{y\\in F\\\\\\|y\\|_S\\ge1}}\n\\left(\\max_{\\pm}\\|x\\pm ty\\|_S-1\\right),\n\\]\none therefore has\n\\[\n\\delta_{X_S}^{\\max}(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/64}-1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finite-support \\(p\\) arbitrarily close to \\(x\\), let \\(C\\) be its finite ancestral closure, and put \\(F=\\ker P_C\\). Theorem 25 gives, uniformly for \\(y\\in F\\) with \\(\\|y\\|_S\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_S\n\\ge\\sqrt{\\|p\\|_S^2+t^2/64}-\\|x-p\\|_S.\n\\]\nAs \\(p\\to x\\), the right side tends to \\(\\sqrt{1+t^2/64}\\). Coordinate continuity makes \\(F\\) closed and finite codimensional. Taking the defining extrema proves both assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 27", "orig": "Corollary 9.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal T=\\bigsqcup_{h\\ge1}T_h\\), with \\(T_h=\\mathbb N^{\\le h}\\). Nodes in distinct trees are incomparable.</p><p class=\"li\">A finite family of nonempty segments \\((S_i)\\) is incomparable if every node of \\(S_i\\) is incomparable with every node of \\(S_j\\) whenever \\(i\\ne j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\|g\\|_{Y_I}=\n  \\sup_{(S_i)\\text{ incomparable}}\n  \\left(\\sum_i\\left|\\sum_{v\\in S_i}g(v)\\right|^2\\right)^{1/2}\n  \\]\n  on \\(c_{00}(\\mathcal T)\\), let \\(Y_I\\) be its completion, and put \\(X_I=Y_I^*\\), with norm \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(e_v\\in X_I\\) be evaluation at \\(v\\), and \\(z_S=\\sum_{v\\in S}e_v\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The real space \\(X_I\\) is reflexive and admits no equivalent AUC norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 18 gives reflexivity and, at every finite height,\n\\[\nv_{s^\\frown j}=v_s+e_{s^\\frown j},\\qquad\n\\|v_s\\|_I=\\|e_{s^\\frown j}\\|_I=1,\\qquad\ne_{s^\\frown j}\\to0\\text{ weakly}.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 2 with all three bounds equal to one. For an equivalent norm\n\\(a\\|\\cdot\\|_I\\le N\\le b\\|\\cdot\\|_I\\), the obstruction scale is \\(a/(2b)\\). If its modulus there were \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), the selection argument would yield\n\\[\nN(v_{s^\\frown j})\\ge(1+\\delta/4)N(v_s)\n\\]\nat successive levels, and hence \\(a(1+\\delta/4)^h\\le b\\) for arbitrarily large \\(h\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 28", "orig": "Theorem 9.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In \\(X_I\\), let \\(E\\subseteq\\mathcal T\\) be finite and prefix closed, and let \\(P_E\\) and \\(Q_E=I-P_E\\) be coordinate restrictions to \\(E\\) and its complement.", "statement": "<p>In \\(X_I\\), let \\(E\\subseteq\\mathcal T\\) be finite and prefix closed, and let \\(P_E\\) and \\(Q_E=I-P_E\\) be coordinate restrictions to \\(E\\) and its complement.</p><p>If\n\\[\nP_Ex=x,\\qquad \\|x+y\\|_I\\le1,\\qquad \\|x-y\\|_I\\le1,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ey\\|_I\\le(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6)\\sqrt{1-\\|x\\|_I}.\n\\]\nIn particular, if \\(Q_Ey=y\\), this bounds \\(\\|y\\|_I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Convexity gives \\(\\|x\\|_I\\le1\\). Choose \\(f\\in Y_I\\), supported on \\(E\\), with\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{Y_I}\\le1,\\qquad x(f)=\\|x\\|_I.\n\\]\nHahn–Banach gives a norming element of \\(X_I^*\\), reflexivity identifies it with \\(Y_I\\), and applying \\(P_E\\) preserves the pairing without increasing its norm. For \\(x=0\\), take \\(f=0\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 19 to represented atoms. Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). By Proposition 18, choose a finite probability distribution of atoms approximating each endpoint \\(x+\\sigma y\\) within \\(\\varepsilon\\), where \\(\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\) is fair. Then\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbb EA-x\\|_I\\le\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbb E(\\sigma A)-y\\|_I\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\le\\bar d:=\\mathbb Ed(A)\\le1-\\|x\\|_I+\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nContractivity, \\(Q_Ex=0\\), and the replacement bound give\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbb E\\widehat A\\|_I\\le\\varepsilon+\\sqrt{2\\bar d},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbb E(\\sigma\\widehat A)-Q_Ey\\|_I\n\\le\\varepsilon+\\sqrt{2\\bar d}.\n\\]</p><p>For the exit representation in Proposition 19, put\n\\[\nm_q=\\mathbb Er_q^A,\\qquad b_q=\\mathbb E|r_q^A|.\n\\]\nApply its opposite-sign inequality to independent unconditional copies \\(A,A'\\). Independence gives\n\\[\n2\\sum_q\\mathbb E(r_q^A)_+\\mathbb E(r_q^A)_-\\le2\\bar d.\n\\]\nSince\n\\(b_q^2-m_q^2=4\\mathbb E(r_q^A)_+\\mathbb E(r_q^A)_-\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_qb_q^2\\le\\sum_qm_q^2+4\\bar d.\n\\]\nAt fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\) all distributions and supports are finite.</p><p>Arrays supported on the antichain of exits have \\(Y_I\\)-norm equal to their Euclidean norm. The coefficient of \\(\\mathbb E\\widehat A\\) at \\(q\\) is \\(m_q\\), so\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_qm_q^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le\\|\\mathbb E\\widehat A\\|_I.\n\\]\nFor \\(g\\in Y_I\\) with \\(\\|g\\|_{Y_I}\\le1\\), define\n\\[\nL_q=\\sup_{t\\succeq q}|z_{[q,t]}(g)|.\n\\]\nSegments selected at finitely many distinct exits are incomparable. Approximating their suprema and then increasing the finite exit set gives \\(\\sum_qL_q^2\\le1\\), without attainment. Therefore\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E(\\sigma\\widehat A)(g)|\n\\le\\sum_qb_qL_q\\le\\left(\\sum_qb_q^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nTake the supremum over \\(g\\) and combine:\n\\[\n\\|Q_Ey\\|_I\n\\le\\varepsilon+\\sqrt{2\\bar d}\n+\\left((\\varepsilon+\\sqrt{2\\bar d})^2+4\\bar d\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe right side increases with \\(\\bar d\\). Substitute\n\\(\\bar d\\le1-\\|x\\|_I+\\varepsilon\\), and let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). The two limiting contributions are\n\\(\\sqrt{2(1-\\|x\\|_I)}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{6(1-\\|x\\|_I)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 29", "orig": "Corollary 9.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(0<c\\le1\\), and suppose \\(x,u_j\\in X_I\\), \\(j\\in\\mathbb N\\), satisfy \\( \\|x+u_j\\|_I,\\ \\|x-u_j\\|_I\\le1, \\qquad \\|u_j-u_k\\|_I\\ge2c\\quad(j\\ne k). \\) With \\(K=\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6\\), \\( \\|x\\|_I\\le1-(c/K)^2<1. \\)", "statement": "<p>Let \\(0&lt;c\\le1\\), and suppose \\(x,u_j\\in X_I\\), \\(j\\in\\mathbb N\\), satisfy\n\\[\n\\|x+u_j\\|_I,\\ \\|x-u_j\\|_I\\le1,\n\\qquad\n\\|u_j-u_k\\|_I\\ge2c\\quad(j\\ne k).\n\\]\nWith \\(K=\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6\\),\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_I\\le1-(c/K)^2&lt;1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;c\\), and use Proposition 18 to choose finite prefix-closed \\(E\\) with \\(\\|Q_Ex\\|_I&lt;\\eta\\). The sequence \\((u_j)\\) is bounded because\n\\[\nu_j=\\frac{(x+u_j)-(x-u_j)}2.\n\\]\nFinite rank gives distinct \\(j,k\\) such that, for \\(w=(u_j-u_k)/2\\),\n\\[\n\\|P_Ew\\|_I&lt;\\eta,\\qquad \\|w\\|_I\\ge c.\n\\]\nThe vector \\(x+w\\) is the midpoint of \\(x+u_j\\) and \\(x-u_k\\), and \\(x-w\\) is the midpoint of the other two endpoints. Hence \\(\\|x\\pm w\\|_I\\le1\\), so\n\\[\n\\|P_Ex\\pm Q_Ew\\|_I\\le1+2\\eta,\\qquad\n\\|Q_Ew\\|_I\\ge c-\\eta.\n\\]\nApply Theorem 28 after division by \\(1+2\\eta\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{c-\\eta}{1+2\\eta}\n\\le K\\sqrt{1-\\frac{\\|P_Ex\\|_I}{1+2\\eta}}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|P_Ex-x\\|_I&lt;\\eta\\), letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) gives\n\\(c\\le K\\sqrt{1-\\|x\\|_I}\\), which is the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “Paired inward tests.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use any one of the four potential norms of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>Use any one of the four potential norms of Theorem 1. A finite initial set \\(D\\subseteq T\\) contains the root and all predecessors of its nodes. Put\n\\[\nF_D=\\{y\\in X_K:y_s=0\\text{ for }s\\in D\\cap I\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(x\\) is supported in \\(D\\cap I\\), \\(y\\in F_D\\), \\(t&gt;0\\), and \\(0\\le\\theta\\le1/2\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x+ty\\|_K+\\|x-ty\\|_K}{2}\n\\ge\\sqrt{1-\\theta^2}\\,\\|x\\|_K+\\frac{t\\theta}{2}\\|y\\|_K.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Restricting a test to \\(D\\) preserves every constraint: quadratic sums decrease and a path sum freezes when the path leaves \\(D\\). The tests supported on \\(D\\cap I\\) form a compact finite-dimensional set. Choose such an \\(f\\) with \\(f(x)=\\|x\\|_K\\), and let \\(g\\in K\\).</p><p>On an exit component, let \\(r\\) be its last ancestor in \\(D\\), put \\(c=P_f(r)\\), and set\n\\[\nR(s)=P_g(s)-P_g(r),\\qquad |R(s)|\\le2.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\sigma=-1\\) if \\(c\\ge0\\), and \\(\\sigma=1\\) otherwise. Define\n\\[\nU(s)=\\sigma(\\sigma R(s))_+,\\qquad\nV(s)=\\sigma(-\\sigma R(s))_+,\n\\]\nand \\(U=V=0\\) on \\(D\\). Both potentials point inward from \\(c\\), and \\(U-V=R\\).</p><p>Let \\(u,v\\) be their increments, with root coefficient zero when appropriate. Signed positive part is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz and fixes zero. On internal and first-exit edges,\n\\[\n|u_s|,|v_s|\\le|g_s|,\\qquad u_s-v_s=g_s\\quad(s\\notin D).\n\\]\nThus each relevant quadratic constraint is at most one. The potentials are bounded by two, so \\(u/2,v/2\\in K\\). Coefficient domination preserves finite support for \\(K_a\\) and square summability for \\(K_g\\). Their evaluations extend continuously, and\n\\[\n(u-v)(y)=g(y)\n\\]\non \\(F_D\\), first on finite tails and then by density.</p><p>Put \\(a=\\sqrt{1-\\theta^2}\\). The potentials of \\(af+\\theta u\\) and \\(af+\\theta v\\) on the component lie in\n\\[\n[ac-2\\theta,ac]\\quad(c\\ge0),\\qquad\n[ac,ac+2\\theta]\\quad(c&lt;0),\n\\]\nboth contained in \\([-1,1]\\). On any quadratic group \\(B\\), disjoint head and tail supports give\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in B}|af_s+\\theta u_s|^2\n=a^2\\sum_{s\\in B\\cap D}|f_s|^2+\n\\theta^2\\sum_{s\\in B\\setminus D}|u_s|^2\\le1.\n\\]\nThe same holds for \\(v\\). Hence both combined tests belong to \\(K\\).</p><p>Evaluate \\(x+ty\\) against \\(af+\\theta u\\) and \\(x-ty\\) against \\(af+\\theta v\\). Adding yields\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x+ty\\|_K+\\|x-ty\\|_K}{2}\n\\ge a\\|x\\|_K+\\frac{t\\theta}{2}g(y).\n\\]\nTake the supremum over \\(g\\in K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(X\\) be a real Banach space.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X\\) be a real Banach space. Suppose \\(0&lt;m\\le M&lt;\\infty\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), and for every \\(h\\ge1\\) there is a tree\n\\[\n(p_s)_{s\\in\\mathbb N^{\\le h}}\\subseteq X\n\\]\nsuch that \\(m\\le\\|p_s\\|\\le M\\). At every nonterminal \\(s\\), suppose\n\\[\nd_{s,n}=p_{s^\\frown n}-p_s\n\\]\nis weakly null as \\(n\\to\\infty\\), with \\(\\|d_{s,n}\\|\\ge c\\).</p><p>If \\(\\alpha\\|\\cdot\\|\\le N\\le\\beta\\|\\cdot\\|\\), then\n\\[\n\\overline\\delta_N\\!\\left(\\frac{\\alpha c}{2\\beta M}\\right)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(t_0=\\alpha c/(2\\beta M)\\), and suppose\n\\(0&lt;\\gamma&lt;\\overline\\delta_N(t_0)\\). At a node \\(p_s\\), choose a finite-codimensional \\(F\\) such that\n\\[\nN(p_s+w)\\ge(1+\\gamma)N(p_s)\n\\quad(w\\in F,\\ N(w)=t_0N(p_s)).\n\\]\nConvexity on a ray gives the same inequality when \\(N(w)\\ge t_0N(p_s)\\).</p><p>Since \\(X/F\\) is finite dimensional and \\(d_{s,n}\\to0\\) weakly, choose \\(w_n\\in F\\) with\n\\(N(w_n-d_{s,n})\\to0\\). Eventually\n\\[\nN(w_n)\\ge\\alpha c/2\\ge t_0N(p_s).\n\\]\nChoose the approximation error at most \\(\\gamma N(p_s)/2\\). The resulting child satisfies\n\\[\nN(p_{s^\\frown n})\\ge(1+\\gamma/2)N(p_s).\n\\]\nChoose \\(h\\) with \\(\\alpha m(1+\\gamma/2)^h&gt;\\beta M\\), and repeat the selection \\(h\\) times. The terminal norm exceeds \\(\\beta M\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Finally, the one-sided asymptotic modulus is nonnegative: at a unit center, intersect with the kernel of a norming functional, on which all perturbed norms are at least one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Section 3.1, “One of two inward parts”; these estimates have no separate rendered theorem label", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the setting and inward increments \\(u,v\\) of Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>Use the setting and inward increments \\(u,v\\) of Proposition 1. For \\(0\\le\\lambda\\le1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{f+\\lambda u}{\\sqrt{1+\\lambda^2}},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{f+\\lambda v}{\\sqrt{1+\\lambda^2}}\n\\in K,\n\\qquad\n\\max\\{|u(y)|,|v(y)|\\}\\ge|g(y)|/2.\n\\]</p><p>For finite unit \\(x\\), \\(f(x)=1\\), \\(y\\in F_D\\), and \\(\\|y\\|_K\\ge1\\), the single-part choice gives\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_K\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}\n\\quad(0&lt;t&lt;1).\n\\]\nBoth endpoints are at least one, so the finite-center average gain is\n\\[\nD(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1}{2}.\n\\]\nApproximation within \\(D(t)/2\\) gives gain \\(D(t)/2\\) at completed centers.</p><p>Using instead \\(\\sqrt{1-\\theta^2}f+\\theta q\\), with \\(q\\in\\{u,v\\}\\), gives the global-model completed-space average gain \\(t^2/256\\) by the choices described in the proof.</p>", "proof": "<p>The potentials \\(c+\\lambda U,c+\\lambda V\\) lie in \\([-1,1]\\), and each quadratic sum is at most \\(1+\\lambda^2\\). Division by \\(\\sqrt{1+\\lambda^2}\\) enforces the quadratic bound without losing the path or support conditions. The identity \\((u-v)(y)=g(y)\\) gives the retained-evaluation bound.</p><p>At the favorable endpoint,\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_K\n\\ge\\frac{1+\\lambda t|g(y)|/2}{\\sqrt{1+\\lambda^2}}.\n\\]\nSet \\(\\lambda=t/2\\) and take the supremum over \\(g\\). The head test gives one for each endpoint. Approximation loses at most the distance between centers in their average.</p><p>For the second normalization, choose \\(|g(y)|\\ge\\|y\\|_K/2\\), so one \\(q\\) satisfies\n\\(|q(y)|\\ge\\|y\\|_K/4\\). With \\(\\theta=t/8\\),\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|_K\n\\ge\\sqrt{1-\\theta^2}+t\\theta\\|y\\|_K/4\n\\ge1+t^2/64.\n\\]\nBoth endpoints remain at least one. In the global model this yields finite-center average gain \\(t^2/128\\); approximation within \\(t^2/256\\) gives the asserted completed-space gain. Continuity applies also to globally constrained tests with infinite support.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Section 3.2, “Four pieces classified by attachment sign”; no separate rendered result label", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the potential models of Theorem 1, a finite initial \\(D\\), a head test \\(f\\), and a test \\(g\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the potential models of Theorem 1, a finite initial \\(D\\), a head test \\(f\\), and a test \\(g\\). Classify exit components by \\(i=1\\) when their inherited value \\(c\\ge0\\), and \\(i=-1\\) otherwise. On each class take the potentials \\(R_+\\) and \\((-R)_+\\), zero elsewhere. Their increment families \\(g_{i,+},g_{i,-}\\) satisfy\n\\[\n|g_{i,\\pm}(s)|\\le|g(s)|,\\qquad\ng1_{T\\setminus D}=\\sum_{i=\\pm1}(g_{i,+}-g_{i,-}),\n\\qquad g_{i,\\pm}/2\\in K.\n\\]\nOne piece retains at least \\(|g(y)|/4\\).</p><p>These choices give completed-space averaged midpoint gains\n\\[\nt^2/1024\\quad\\text{in }X_{K_p},\n\\qquad\n19t^2/81920\\quad\\text{in }X_{K_g},\n\\qquad0&lt;t&lt;1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficient bounds hold on first-exit edges because the relative potential starts at zero. Each piece has potential bounded by two, so division by two gives a test. The coefficient identity extends from finite tails to \\(F_D\\). One of the four evaluations has magnitude at least \\(|g(y)|/4\\).</p><p>For \\(X_{K_p}\\), fix a unit \\(x\\), and put\n\\[\n\\lambda=t/16,\\qquad a=\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^2},\\qquad\n\\eta=t^2/2048.\n\\]\nChoose finite \\(x_0\\) within \\(\\eta\\), a supporting initial \\(D\\), and a head test with\n\\(f(x_0)\\ge1-2\\eta\\). Intersect \\(F_D\\) with the kernel of a functional norming \\(x\\). For a direction \\(y\\) of norm at least one, choose \\(g\\) with\n\\(|g(y)|\\ge\\|y\\|/2\\), and select a piece \\(q\\), oriented inward from its attachment class. Then\n\\[\n|q(y)|\\ge\\|y\\|/8,\\qquad af+\\lambda q\\in K_p.\n\\]\nThe test evaluates \\(x\\) at least at \\(a-3\\eta\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|\n\\ge1-\\lambda^2-3\\eta+t\\lambda/8\n=1+5t^2/2048\\ge1+t^2/512.\n\\]\nThe norming functional gives one at both endpoints, proving average gain \\(t^2/1024\\).</p><p>For \\(X_{K_g}\\), put \\(\\alpha=t/64\\), \\(\\eta=\\alpha^2/10\\), and approximate \\(x\\) within \\(\\eta/10\\) by \\(x_0\\) supported in \\(D\\). A head norming test satisfies \\(f(x)\\ge1-\\eta\\). Choose an inward piece \\(q\\) with \\(|q(y)|\\ge1/8\\). Since its functional norm is at most two,\n\\[\n|q(x)|\\le2\\eta/10.\n\\]\nThe test\n\\[\nH=\\frac{f+\\alpha q}{\\sqrt{1+\\alpha^2}}\n\\]\nis admissible. Both endpoint norms are at least \\(1-\\eta\\), while\n\\[\n\\max_\\pm\\|x\\pm ty\\|\n\\ge\\frac{1-\\eta-2\\alpha\\eta/10+\\alpha t/8}{\\sqrt{1+\\alpha^2}}\n\\ge1+2\\alpha^2.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\alpha t/8=8\\alpha^2\\), and the numerator\n\\(1+(79/10)\\alpha^2-\\alpha^3/50\\) dominates\n\\((1+2\\alpha^2)(1+\\alpha^2/2)\\) for \\(0\\le\\alpha\\le1/64\\).\nThe average gain is at least \\(19\\alpha^2/20=19t^2/81920\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Section 3.3, “Independent component selection”; no separate rendered result label", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the antichain model \\(K_a\\), retain the finite-head notation of Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>In the antichain model \\(K_a\\), retain the finite-head notation of Proposition 1. Because every test is finitely supported, selections may be made independently on its finitely many nonzero exit components.</p><p>The two selections in the proof yield completed-space average gains\n\\[\n\\frac{433}{640000}t^2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\frac{t^2}{1024},\n\\qquad0&lt;t&lt;1,\n\\]\nrespectively.</p>", "proof": "<p>First split\n\\[\nR=2\\bigl(R_+/2-(-R)_+/2\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe increment families \\(g^+,g^-\\) have coefficients at most \\(|g_s|/2\\) and component potentials in \\([0,1]\\), so both belong to \\(K_a\\). Choose one, \\(k\\), with \\(|k(y)|\\ge|g(y)|/4\\). If \\(q_j\\) is its evaluation on component \\(j\\), then\n\\[\n\\sum_j|q_j|\\ge|g(y)|/4.\n\\]\nChoose signs \\(d_j\\) pointing inward. Put \\(r=t/32\\), \\(s=\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\). Adjoining either \\(rd_jk\\) or zero componentwise to \\(sf\\) preserves the path bound and every antichain square sum. Retain \\(d_jq_j&gt;0\\) for the plus endpoint and \\(d_jq_j&lt;0\\) for the minus endpoint. Then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x_0+ty\\|+\\|x_0-ty\\|}{2}\n\\ge sf(x_0)+\\frac{tr}{2}\\sum_j|q_j|\n\\ge sf(x_0)+tr|g(y)|/8.\n\\]\nFor a unit completed center choose \\(x_0\\) within\n\\(\\eta=t^2/10000\\), \\(f(x_0)\\ge1-2\\eta\\), and \\(g(y)\\ge1/2\\).\nReturning to \\(x\\) loses at most \\(\\eta\\). Since \\(s\\ge1-r^2\\), the excess is at least\n\\[\ntr/16-r^2-3\\eta=\\frac{433}{640000}t^2.\n\\]</p><p>For the second choice, split \\(R\\) into its positive and negative parts on each component. Select the part retaining at least half the full component evaluation, and orient it inward. The selected increments \\(h_j\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_j|h_j(y)|\\ge|g(y)|/2.\n\\]\nRetain the positive or negative group with larger total magnitude. Their sum \\(h\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|h(y)|\\ge|g(y)|/4,\\qquad |h_s|\\le|g_s|,\\qquad h/2\\in K_a.\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le\\alpha\\le1/2\\), the test\n\\(\\sqrt{1-\\alpha^2}f+\\alpha h\\) is admissible.</p><p>Put \\(\\alpha=t/16\\), \\(\\varepsilon=t^2/2048\\). Choose finite \\(x_0\\) within \\(\\varepsilon\\) and a finite test with \\(f(x)&gt;1-\\varepsilon\\); enlarge \\(D\\) to contain both supports. Choose \\(g(y)&gt;\\|y\\|/2\\), obtaining \\(|h(y)|\\ge1/8\\), and use\n\\(|h(x)|\\le2\\varepsilon\\). The favorable endpoint is at least\n\\[\n1-\\alpha^2-(1+2\\alpha)\\varepsilon+\\alpha t/8,\n\\]\nand the other is at least \\(1-\\varepsilon\\). The paper's stated average estimate is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha t/8-\\alpha^2-(2+2\\alpha)\\varepsilon}{2}\n\\ge\\frac{5t^2}{4096}\\ge\\frac{t^2}{1024}.\n\\]\nThe subspace is chosen before the direction \\(y\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(T_n=\\mathbb N^{\\le n}\\), \\(H_n=\\ell_2(T_n)\\), define \\( b_s=\\sum_{r\\preceq s}e_r,\\qquad B_n\\mu=\\sum_s\\mu_sb_s, \\) …", "statement": "<p>For \\(T_n=\\mathbb N^{\\le n}\\), \\(H_n=\\ell_2(T_n)\\), define\n\\[\nb_s=\\sum_{r\\preceq s}e_r,\\qquad\nB_n\\mu=\\sum_s\\mu_sb_s,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_n(x)=\\inf_{x=h+B_n\\mu}(\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_1),\n\\qquad\np_n(f)=\\max\\left\\{\\|f\\|_2,\\sup_s\\left|\\sum_{r\\preceq s}f_r\\right|\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(B_n:\\ell_1(T_n)\\to H_n\\) is bounded by \\(\\sqrt{n+1}\\), \\(X_n=(H_n,L_n)\\) is complete, and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x\\|_2}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\\le L_n(x)\\le\\|x\\|_2,\n\\qquad L_n(x)=\\sup_{p_n(f)\\le1}\\langle f,x\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe dual norm is exactly \\(p_n\\), and \\(|x_s|\\le L_n(x)\\).</p><p>Restricting \\(\\mu\\) to finite support does not change the infimum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(\\|b_s\\|_2\\le\\sqrt{n+1}\\), the series for \\(B_n\\mu\\) converges absolutely and has the asserted bound. Truncate \\(\\mu\\) in \\(\\ell_1\\) and absorb the vanishing remainder into \\(h\\) to obtain finite path coefficients.</p><p>The choice \\(h=x,\\mu=0\\) gives the upper estimate. Every representation satisfies\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_2\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\sqrt{n+1}\\|\\mu\\|_1\n\\le\\sqrt{n+1}(\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_1).\n\\]\nScaling and adding near-optimal representations give homogeneity and the triangle inequality. The lower estimate gives definiteness, completeness, and density of finite arrays.</p><p>If \\(p_n(f)\\le1\\), absolute convergence gives\n\\[\n|\\langle f,x\\rangle|\n\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\sum_s|\\mu_s|\n\\left|\\sum_{r\\preceq s}f_r\\right|\n\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_1.\n\\]\nConversely, Hahn–Banach supplies a functional \\(\\phi\\) norming a nonzero \\(x\\) for \\(L_n\\). Since \\(L_n\\le\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\), write \\(\\phi(z)=\\langle f,z\\rangle\\), with \\(\\|f\\|_2\\le1\\). Because \\(L_n(b_s)\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{r\\preceq s}f_r\\right|=|\\phi(b_s)|\\le1.\n\\]\nThis proves the polar formula. For arbitrary \\(f\\), the representation estimate bounds its dual norm by \\(p_n(f)\\); testing on the Hilbert ball and the signed paths, all contained in the \\(L_n\\)-ball, gives the reverse bounds. Signed coordinate tests give \\(|x_s|\\le L_n(x)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "the finite-head discussion in Section 5.1 and the structural part of Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(X=(\\bigoplus_nX_n)_{\\ell_2}\\), with the blocks of Proposition 6.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X=(\\bigoplus_nX_n)_{\\ell_2}\\), with the blocks of Proposition 6.</p><p>Then \\(X\\) is reflexive with separable dual\n\\[\nX^*=\\left(\\bigoplus_n(H_n,p_n)\\right)_{\\ell_2}.\n\\]\nInitial coordinate restrictions are contractive on the blocks, their duals, and \\(X\\). Finite-head restrictions converge strongly to the identity, and\n\\[\n\\|P_C-P_A\\|\\le2\\qquad(A\\subseteq C\\text{ initial}).\n\\]\nFor a finite initial \\(A\\subseteq T_n\\), its induced unit ball is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{conv}\n\\bigl(B_{\\ell_2(A)}\\cup\\{\\pm b_s:s\\in A\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\nOn the entire block it is the norm-closed convex hull of the Hilbert ball and signed paths.</p><p>Every coordinate and root path has norm one, and each fixed sibling sequence is weakly null.</p>", "proof": "<p>Restricting a root path to an initial set gives zero or the path ending at its last retained ancestor. Grouping path coefficients at this endpoint cannot increase their \\(\\ell_1\\)-norm. This proves primal contractivity. On the dual, restriction decreases the Hilbert norm and freezes each prefix sum, proving dual contractivity.</p><p>The finite-head convex hull is compact, symmetric, and has polar precisely the supported tests of Proposition 6. Finite-dimensional separation identifies the balls. On the whole block, finite truncation of near-optimal representations and a scaling tending to one give the closed-convex-hull description.</p><p>Each block is an equivalently normed Hilbert space. A Cauchy sequence in the outer sum has component limits; its estimates pass first to finite sets of components and then to the entire sum. Cauchy–Schwarz gives the displayed dual pairing. Conversely, restriction of a functional to finitely many blocks, testing on almost norming vectors with proportional scalar weights, bounds the sum of squared block dual norms. Density gives the representation and norm equality. Applying the same argument to the dual proves reflexivity. Block truncation and Hilbert approximation give separability of the dual.</p><p>To exhaust a vector by finite heads, first truncate the block sum, then approximate the remaining components by finite arrays and take their ancestor hulls. The bound on \\(P_C-P_A\\) follows by the triangle inequality.</p><p>The cost formula gives \\(L_n(e_s),L_n(b_s)\\le1\\); the dual vector \\(e_s\\), with \\(p_n(e_s)=1\\), evaluates to one on both. Weak nullity follows from square summability of each fixed dual array.</p><p>At height one, the paths are \\(e_o\\) and \\(e_o+e_s\\), so the ball is the one described by Dilworth et al. The infinite-height global norm restricts to \\(L_n\\), by restriction and zero extension of tests, but this does not identify the infinite-height completion with the outer sum.</p><p>Finally, the quadratic cost\n\\[\nQ_n(x)=\\inf_{x=h+B_n\\mu}\n(\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\mu\\|_1^2)^{1/2}\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(Q_n\\le L_n\\le\\sqrt2Q_n\\), and is not \\(L_n\\):\n\\[\nL_n(e_o)=1,\\qquad Q_n(e_o)=1/\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nSplitting the root equally gives the upper bound for the latter equality, and\n\\(1\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_1\\le\\sqrt2(\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\mu\\|_1^2)^{1/2}\\)\ngives the lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the linear path space \\(X\\), let \\(A\\) be a finite head, and suppose \\( \\|h\\|_*,\\|g\\|_*\\le1,\\qquad P_Ah=h. \\) There are \\(r_1,r_2\\in X^*\\), vanishing on \\(A\\), such that \\( r_1-r_2=(I-P_A)g,\\qquad \\|r_l\\|_*\\le2, \\) …", "statement": "<p>In the linear path space \\(X\\), let \\(A\\) be a finite head, and suppose\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_*,\\|g\\|_*\\le1,\\qquad P_Ah=h.\n\\]\nThere are \\(r_1,r_2\\in X^*\\), vanishing on \\(A\\), such that\n\\[\nr_1-r_2=(I-P_A)g,\\qquad \\|r_l\\|_*\\le2,\n\\]\nand, for \\(l=1,2\\) and \\(\\lambda,\\mu\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda h+\\mu r_l\\|_*^2\\le\\lambda^2+4\\mu^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>In block \\(n\\), write \\(A_n=A\\cap T_n\\), and set\n\\[\nH(s)=\\sum_{r\\preceq s}h_{n,r},\\qquad\nV(s)=\\sum_{\\substack{r\\preceq s\\\\r\\notin A_n}}g_{n,r},\n\\qquad a_n=p_n(h_n),\\quad c_n=p_n(g_n).\n\\]\nThen \\(|H|\\le a_n\\), \\(|V|\\le2c_n\\), and \\(V=0\\) on \\(A_n\\). Define\n\\[\n(V_1,V_2)=\n\\begin{cases}\n(\\min(V,0),-\\max(V,0)),&amp;H\\ge0,\\\\\n(\\max(V,0),-\\min(V,0)),&amp;H&lt;0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nLet \\(r_{l,n,s}=V_l(s)-V_l(s^-)\\), with predecessor value zero at the root.</p><p>Outside \\(A_n\\), the head sum \\(H\\) is constant on each remaining branch. The clipping maps are \\(1\\)-Lipschitz, including at first exits, so\n\\[\n|r_{l,n,s}|\\le|g_{n,s}|\\quad(s\\notin A_n).\n\\]\nAlso \\(V_1-V_2=V\\). Disjoint head and tail supports give\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda h_n+\\mu r_{l,n}\\|_2^2\\le\\lambda^2a_n^2+\\mu^2c_n^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(HV_l\\le0\\),\n\\[\n|\\lambda H+\\mu V_l|\\le\\max\\{\\lambda a_n,2\\mu c_n\\}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\np_n(\\lambda h_n+\\mu r_{l,n})^2\n\\le\\lambda^2a_n^2+4\\mu^2c_n^2.\n\\]\nSum over \\(n\\). Taking \\(\\lambda=0,\\mu=1\\) gives continuity and the norm bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 5.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(T_n\\), an initial head \\(A\\), and the complementary forest \\(U=T_n\\setminus A\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(T_n\\), an initial head \\(A\\), and the complementary forest \\(U=T_n\\setminus A\\). Define\n\\[\nb_t^U=\\sum_{\\substack{v\\in U\\\\v\\preceq t}}e_v,\\qquad\nB_U\\alpha=\\sum_{t\\in U}\\alpha(t)b_t^U,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_U(z)=\\inf_{z=h+B_U\\alpha}(\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\alpha\\|_1).\n\\]\nIf \\(0\\le\\nu\\le\\mu\\) in \\(\\ell_1(U)\\), then\n\\[\nL_U(B_U\\nu)\\le L_U(B_U\\mu).\n\\]\nChanging signs on whole trees of \\(U\\) is an isometry for \\(L_U\\). If \\(v\\) is supported on \\(U\\), then\n\\[\nL_n(v)\\le2L_U(v|_U).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All intrinsic paths have length at most \\(n+1\\), so the cost is Hilbert-equivalent. Whole-tree sign changes preserve both representation costs. After zero extension,\n\\(b_t^U=b_t-P_Ab_t\\), of \\(L_n\\)-cost at most two, proving the comparison.</p><p>Choose a norming functional \\(\\phi\\in\\ell_2(U)\\) for \\(B_U\\nu\\), with\n\\[\n\\|\\phi\\|_2\\le1,\\qquad\nF_t=\\sum_{\\substack{v\\in U\\\\v\\preceq t}}\\phi(v),\\qquad |F_t|\\le1.\n\\]\nSet the potential at each nonexistent root parent equal to zero and define\n\\[\n\\psi(v)=|F_v|-|F_{\\operatorname{par}(v)}|.\n\\]\nThen \\(|\\psi(v)|\\le|\\phi(v)|\\), and its intrinsic prefix sums are \\(|F_t|\\), so it also has dual norm at most one. Absolute convergence gives\n\\[\nL_U(B_U\\nu)=\\sum_t\\nu(t)F_t\n\\le\\sum_t\\mu(t)|F_t|\n=\\langle B_U\\mu,\\psi\\rangle\n\\le L_U(B_U\\mu).\n\\]\nThis is monotonicity of positive path coefficients, not general coordinatewise monotonicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 5.8, “Subtree quotas.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On a forest of finite height, let \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) be nonnegative finitely supported endpoint masses.", "statement": "<p>On a forest of finite height, let \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) be nonnegative finitely supported endpoint masses. For each vertex \\(v\\), let \\(K_v\\) be its descendant set, and put\n\\[\nG(v)=\\mu(K_v),\\qquad W(v)=\\nu(K_v).\n\\]\nThere is \\(0\\le\\rho\\le\\mu\\) such that\n\\[\n\\rho(K_v)\\ge\\min\\{G(v),W(v)\\}\\quad\\text{for every }v,\n\\qquad \\|\\rho\\|_1\\le\\|\\nu\\|_1.\n\\]\nThe last inequality holds separately in each tree.</p>", "proof": "<p>Only the finite ancestor hull of the supports matters. Write\n\\(q(v)=\\min\\{G(v),W(v)\\}\\). Then\n\\[\nq(v)\\le G(v),\\qquad\n\\sum_{w\\text{ child of }v}q(w)\\le q(v).\n\\]\nProcess vertices from leaves towards roots. At \\(v\\), the already marked mass in its child subtrees totals \\(\\sum_wq(w)\\). Mark enough additional, previously unmarked \\(\\mu\\)-mass below \\(v\\) to reach \\(q(v)\\); fractions are allowed. The two inequalities make this possible. Later steps only increase previously met quotas. The final marked mass in a tree equals its root quota and is at most its total \\(\\nu\\)-mass. Take \\(\\rho\\) to be this marked mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “Removing excess mass.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In one linear path block, let \\(S\\subseteq T\\subseteq T_n\\) be finite initial sets, let \\(z^+,z^-\\in H_n\\), and put \\(z=(z^++z^-)/2\\), \\(U=T\\setminus S\\).", "statement": "<p>In one linear path block, let \\(S\\subseteq T\\subseteq T_n\\) be finite initial sets, let \\(z^+,z^-\\in H_n\\), and put \\(z=(z^++z^-)/2\\), \\(U=T\\setminus S\\).</p><p>For a finite initial \\(R\\), define\n\\[\nd_R(w,u)=w(u)-\\sum_{\\substack{v\\text{ child of }u\\\\v\\in R}}w(v),\n\\qquad\nV_R(w)=\\sum_{u\\in R}|d_R(w,u)|,\n\\qquad V_\\varnothing(w)=0.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\ng=\\frac{V_T(z^+)+V_T(z^-)}2-V_S(z).\n\\]\nThen \\(g\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\nL_n(R_Uz^\\pm)\\le2\\|R_Uz\\|_2+4g.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite summation up the tree gives\n\\[\nR_Rw=\\sum_{u\\in R}d_R(w,u)b_u,\n\\qquad V_R(B_nc)\\le\\|c\\|_1.\n\\]\nIn the latter inequality, collapsed coefficients sum over disjoint endpoint classes.</p><p>If \\(S=\\varnothing\\), then \\(L_n(R_Tz^\\pm)\\le V_T(z^\\pm)\\le2g\\). Otherwise \\(S\\) contains the root. Let \\(\\pi(u)\\) be the deepest ancestor of \\(u\\) in \\(S\\), and choose\n\\[\n\\sigma_sd_S(z,s)=|d_S(z,s)|,\\qquad \\sigma_s\\in\\{-1,1\\}.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nd_T(z^\\pm,u)=\\sigma_{\\pi(u)}(p_u^\\pm-q_u^\\pm),\n\\qquad p_u^\\pm,q_u^\\pm\\ge0,\n\\]\nusing positive and negative parts. Collapsing to \\(S\\) gives\n\\[\ng=2\\sum_{u\\in T}\\frac{q_u^++q_u^-}{2}\\ge0.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(u\\in U\\), all descendants in \\(T\\) have the same \\(\\pi\\)-label. Let \\(P_u^\\pm,Q_u^\\pm\\) be the descendant sums of \\(p^\\pm,q^\\pm\\). With \\(\\operatorname{av}\\) denoting the average over signs,\n\\[\nP_u^\\pm\\le2\\operatorname{av}P_u^\\pm\n\\le2|z(u)|+2\\operatorname{av}Q_u^\\pm.\n\\]\nFor a fixed sign, apply Proposition 10 to\n\\(\\mu_u=p_u^\\pm\\), \\(\\nu_u=q_u^++q_u^-\\) on \\(U\\). It gives \\(0\\le r_u\\le p_u^\\pm\\), total mass at most \\(g\\), with\n\\[\nP_u^\\pm-\\sum_{v\\succeq u}r_v\n\\le(P_u^\\pm-2\\operatorname{av}Q_u^\\pm)_+\n\\le2|z(u)|.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\sum_{u\\in U}q_u^\\pm\\le g\\). Define the remaining positive occupancy\n\\[\nv(u)=\\sigma_{\\pi(u)}\n\\sum_{w\\succeq u}(p_w^\\pm-r_w)\n\\quad(u\\in U),\n\\]\nzero elsewhere. Then \\(|v(u)|\\le2|z(u)|\\), and\n\\[\nR_Uz^\\pm=v+\\sum_{u\\in U}\n\\sigma_{\\pi(u)}(r_u-q_u^\\pm)R_Ub_u.\n\\]\nEach window path has norm at most two. The decomposition therefore costs at most\n\\(2\\|R_Uz\\|_2+2(g+g)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 7.1 and Lemma 7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the quadratic path norms \\( |z|_n=\\inf_{z=h+J_n\\lambda} (\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\lambda\\|_1^2)^{1/2}. \\) Then \\(Z_n\\) and \\(X_q=(\\bigoplus_nZ_n)_{\\ell_2}\\) are Banach and reflexive, finite node support is dense, and …", "statement": "<p>Use the quadratic path norms\n\\[\n|z|_n=\\inf_{z=h+J_n\\lambda}\n(\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\lambda\\|_1^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThen \\(Z_n\\) and \\(X_q=(\\bigoplus_nZ_n)_{\\ell_2}\\) are Banach and reflexive, finite node support is dense, and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|z\\|_2}{\\sqrt{n+2}}\\le|z|_n\\le\\|z\\|_2,\n\\qquad |z_t|\\le\\sqrt2\\,|z|_n.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n1/\\sqrt2\\le|p_t|_n,|e_t|_n\\le1,\n\\qquad |e_o|_n=1/\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe dual norm is\n\\[\n|f|_{n,*}=\n\\left(\\|f\\|_2^2+\n\\sup_{t\\in T_n}\\left|\\sum_{s\\preceq t}f_s\\right|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nH=\\left(\\bigoplus_nH_n\\right)_{\\ell_2},\n\\qquad\nL=\\left(\\bigoplus_n\\ell_1(T_n)\\right)_{\\ell_2}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\|z\\|_q=\\inf_{z=h+J\\lambda}\n(\\|h\\|_H^2+\\|\\lambda\\|_L^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nFor a finite ancestral head \\(A\\), let \\(r(t)\\) be the longest ancestor of \\(t\\) in \\(A\\), using \\(\\perp\\) if absent and \\(p_\\perp=0\\). The folding map \\(R_A\\), sending mass at \\(t\\) to \\(r(t)\\) and discarding it at \\(\\perp\\), is contractive on \\(L\\), and\n\\[\nP_AJ=JR_A,\\qquad\n\\|P_A\\|\\le1,\\qquad \\|Q_A\\|\\le2,\\qquad\n|Q_AJ_n\\lambda|_n\\le2\\|\\lambda\\|_1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Scaling and adding representations gives homogeneity and the triangle inequality. Every representation satisfies\n\\[\n\\|z\\|_2\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\sqrt{n+1}\\|\\lambda\\|_1\n\\le\\sqrt{n+2}(\\|h\\|_2^2+\\|\\lambda\\|_1^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe Hilbert representation gives the upper bound. Thus the norm is complete and Hilbert-equivalent, so \\(Z_n\\) is reflexive. Since\n\\(|z_t|\\le\\|h\\|_2+\\|\\lambda\\|_1\\), the coordinate estimate follows. Single masses represent \\(p_t\\), and Hilbert vectors represent \\(e_t\\). Splitting \\(e_o\\) equally between these representations attains \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), matching the coordinate lower bound.</p><p>Pull a functional \\(f\\) back through\n\\[\nH_n\\oplus_2\\ell_1(T_n)\\longrightarrow Z_n,\\qquad\n(h,\\lambda)\\mapsto h+J_n\\lambda.\n\\]\nIts pullback is \\((f,J_n^*f)\\), of norm\n\\((\\|f\\|_2^2+\\|J_n^*f\\|_\\infty^2)^{1/2}\\).\nCauchy–Schwarz gives the upper bound; choose a Hilbert vector in the direction of \\(f\\), an atomic coordinate approaching the supremum of \\(J_n^*f\\), and optimize their two lengths for the reverse bound. Quotient and pullback norms agree by near-optimal representations. Since\n\\((J_n^*f)(t)=\\sum_{s\\preceq t}f_s\\), this proves the dual formula.</p><p>The outer sum is complete by componentwise convergence and passage through finite partial sums. Its dual is the \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum of component duals, by testing on finite collections of almost norming vectors with Euclidean scalar weights and using Cauchy–Schwarz. Applying this description to the bidual proves reflexivity. Truncating components and then Hilbert coordinates proves finite-support density.</p><p>Component representations chosen with arbitrarily small total error in their squared costs prove the global quotient formula. Here \\(J:L\\to X_q\\) and \\(H\\to X_q\\) are contractions; no uniform map \\(L\\to H\\) is used.</p><p>Finally, absolute sums in each folding fibre cannot increase, and \\(P_Ap_t=p_{r(t)}\\). Project \\(h\\) orthogonally and fold \\(\\lambda\\) in the quotient formula to obtain contractivity of \\(P_A\\). The remaining inequalities follow from \\(Q_A=I-P_A\\) and contractivity of \\(J\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Lemma 7.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a finite ancestral head \\(A\\) in \\(X_q\\), write \\(P=P_A\\), \\(Q=Q_A\\), and suppose \\(Px=x\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a finite ancestral head \\(A\\) in \\(X_q\\), write \\(P=P_A\\), \\(Q=Q_A\\), and suppose \\(Px=x\\). Choose finite-total-cost representations\n\\[\nx\\pm y=h^\\pm+J\\lambda^\\pm.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\bar h=(h^++h^-)/2,\\quad\n\\widetilde h=(h^+-h^-)/2,\\quad\n\\sigma=(\\lambda^++\\lambda^-)/2,\\quad\n\\tau=(\\lambda^+-\\lambda^-)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nM_n=(\\|\\lambda_n^+\\|_1+\\|\\lambda_n^-\\|_1)/2,\\quad\n\\nu_n=(R_A\\sigma)_n,\\quad q_n=\\|\\nu_n\\|_1,\\quad d_n=M_n-q_n.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nC=\\frac12\\sum_{\\epsilon\\in\\{+,-\\}}\n(\\|h^\\epsilon\\|_H^2+\\|\\lambda^\\epsilon\\|_L^2),\n\\qquad E=C-\\|x\\|_q^2.\n\\]\nThen \\(d_n\\ge0\\), \\(x=P\\bar h+J\\nu\\), and\n\\[\n\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\|\\widetilde h\\|_H^2+\n\\sum_n(M_n^2-q_n^2)\\le E,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|Qh^+\\|_H^2+\\|Qh^-\\|_H^2}{2}\n+\\sum_nd_n^2\\le E.\n\\]</p><p>Writing \\(m_n=(|\\lambda_n^+|+|\\lambda_n^-|)/2\\) and\n\\(C_r=\\{t:r(t)=r\\}\\), the loss is\n\\[\nd_n=\\sum_{r\\in A\\cap T_n}\n\\bigl(m_n(C_r)-|\\sigma_n(C_r)|\\bigr)+m_n(C_\\perp).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Absolute convergence gives the fibre formula; every term is nonnegative. The Hilbert parallelogram identity and the scalar square inequality give\n\\[\nC\\ge\\|\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\|\\widetilde h\\|_H^2+\\sum_nM_n^2.\n\\]\nThe representation \\(x=P\\bar h+J\\nu\\) has squared cost\n\\(\\|P\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\sum_nq_n^2\\ge\\|x\\|_q^2\\).\nSubtract and use Hilbert orthogonality to obtain the first inequality. Then use\n\\[\nM_n^2-q_n^2\\ge(M_n-q_n)^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|Qh^+\\|_H^2+\\|Qh^-\\|_H^2}{2}\n=\\|Q\\bar h\\|_H^2+\\|Q\\widetilde h\\|_H^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Stopped flows.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(O=T_n\\setminus A_n\\), where \\(A_n\\) is an ancestral head, and let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be the roots of the connected subtrees of \\(O\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(O=T_n\\setminus A_n\\), where \\(A_n\\) is an ancestral head, and let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be the roots of the connected subtrees of \\(O\\). Suppose \\(V:O\\to[0,\\infty)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nV(t)\\ge\\sum_{c\\text{ child of }t}V(c),\n\\qquad\nB=\\sum_{c\\in\\mathcal R}V(c)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFor signs \\(\\epsilon_t\\) constant on each connected subtree, the vector\n\\(\\epsilon_tV(t)\\), extended by zero on \\(A_n\\), has \\(Z_n\\)-norm at most \\(2B\\).</p><p>The pointwise minimum of two arrays satisfying the child inequality also satisfies it.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\n\\alpha_t=V(t)-\\sum_{c\\text{ child of }t}V(c)\\ge0.\n\\]\nInduction from the last level, using nonnegative sums, gives\n\\[\nV(t)=\\sum_{s\\succeq t}\\alpha_s,\\qquad\n\\sum_{t\\in O}\\alpha_t=B.\n\\]\nThis is induction over the finite height, not over a finite set of children. The vector is\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\in O}\\epsilon_t\\alpha_t(p_t-p_{r(t)}).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 12 this series converges absolutely in \\(Z_n\\) and costs at most \\(2B\\). Coordinate continuity identifies its sum.</p><p>For minima,\n\\[\n\\sum_c\\min\\{V(c),W(c)\\}\n\\le\\min\\left\\{\\sum_cV(c),\\sum_cW(c)\\right\\}\n\\le\\min\\{V(t),W(t)\\}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 7.16", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(x\\in X_q\\) be a finitely supported unit vector.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(x\\in X_q\\) be a finitely supported unit vector. For each \\(n\\) with \\(x_n\\ne0\\), choose a finite nonempty ancestral \\(A_n\\subseteq T_n\\) containing its support, and set\n\\[\nY_x=\\{y\\in X_q:P_{A_n}y_n=0\\text{ whenever }x_n\\ne0\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(Y_x\\) is closed and finite codimensional. If \\(0&lt;e\\le1/8\\), \\(y\\in Y_x\\), and\n\\[\n\\|x+y\\|_q,\\ \\|x-y\\|_q\\le1+e,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\|y\\|_q^2\\le\\frac{519}{2}e^{1/3},\n\\qquad\n\\|y\\|_q\\le17e^{1/6}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The coordinate exclusions defining \\(Y_x\\) are finitely many continuous conditions.</p><p>Put\n\\[\na_n=|x_n|_n,\\quad b_n=|x_n+y_n|_n,\\quad\nc_n=|x_n-y_n|_n,\n\\]\n\\[\nv_n=(b_n^2+c_n^2)/2,\\qquad d_n=v_n-a_n^2.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality gives\n\\[\nd_n\\ge0,\\qquad |y_n|_n^2\\le v_n,\\qquad\n\\sum_na_n^2=1,\\qquad \\sum_nd_n\\le3e.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\eta\\le1\\), let \\(E_\\eta=\\{n:d_n&gt;\\eta a_n^2\\}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\in E_\\eta}|y_n|_n^2\\le3e(1+1/\\eta).\n\\]\nEvery remaining nonzero contribution has \\(a_n&gt;0\\): if \\(a_n=0\\) and \\(y_n\\ne0\\), then \\(d_n=v_n&gt;0\\).</p><p>For \\(n\\notin E_\\eta\\), put \\(m_n=(b_n+c_n)/2\\). If \\(\\eta\\le1/4\\), then\n\\[\nm_n\\ge a_n,\\qquad\nm_n^2+\\left(\\frac{b_n-c_n}{2}\\right)^2=v_n\\le(1+\\eta)a_n^2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\frac{\\max(b_n,c_n)}{a_n}\n\\le\\sqrt{1+\\eta}+\\sqrt\\eta=1+\\delta_\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_\\eta\\le\\frac32\\sqrt\\eta\\le1.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 15.1 to \\(x_n/a_n,y_n/a_n\\), obtaining\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\notin E_\\eta}|y_n|_n^2\n\\le169\\delta_\\eta\\sum_{n\\notin E_\\eta}a_n^2\n\\le\\frac{507}{2}\\sqrt\\eta.\n\\]\nTake \\(\\eta=e^{2/3}\\le1/4\\). The two estimates give\n\\[\n\\|y\\|_q^2\n\\le\\frac{507}{2}e^{1/3}+3e+3e^{1/3}\n\\le\\frac{519}{2}e^{1/3}.\n\\]\nSince \\(519/2&lt;17^2\\), the norm estimate follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.15", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In one quadratic block, let \\(A\\subseteq T_n\\) be finite, nonempty, and ancestral.", "statement": "<p>In one quadratic block, let \\(A\\subseteq T_n\\) be finite, nonempty, and ancestral. Suppose\n\\[\nP_Ax=x,\\qquad P_Ay=0,\\qquad |x|_n=1.\n\\]\nIf \\(0\\le e\\le1\\) and \\(|x\\pm y|_n\\le1+e\\), then\n\\[\n|y|_n\\le(6+\\sqrt2)\\sqrt{(1+e)^2-1}\\le13\\sqrt e.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose endpoint representations with costs below \\(1+e+\\varepsilon\\), and put\n\\(\\kappa=(1+e+\\varepsilon)^2-1\\). Proposition 13 bounds the individual Hilbert tails by \\(\\sqrt{2\\kappa}\\), their sum by \\(2\\sqrt\\kappa\\), and the folded-mass loss by \\(\\sqrt\\kappa\\).</p><p>Because \\(A\\) contains the root, no fibre is discarded. Orient each fibre by the majority sign of the two labeled endpoint arrays. The combined minority mass equals the folded-mass loss. Clip the plus-majority occupancy against the combined minority occupancy. The clipped majority flow and the plus-minority flow each have norm at most \\(2\\sqrt\\kappa\\), by Proposition 14. The remaining majority occupancy is bounded pointwise by the absolute sum of the two Hilbert tails, and therefore has Hilbert norm at most \\(2\\sqrt\\kappa\\). Adding the original plus Hilbert tail gives\n\\[\n|y|_n\\le(6+\\sqrt2)\\sqrt\\kappa.\n\\]\nNo invariance under arbitrary coordinate sign changes is used. Let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). Finally,\n\\[\n(1+e)^2-1\\le3e,\\qquad\n3(6+\\sqrt2)^2=114+36\\sqrt2&lt;169.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the nonnegative start-mass costs \\(q_t,M_n,M\\) defined in Theorem 25, the costs are monotone, positively homogeneous, and subadditive.", "statement": "<p>For the nonnegative start-mass costs \\(q_t,M_n,M\\) defined in Theorem 25, the costs are monotone, positively homogeneous, and subadditive. On \\(T_n\\),\n\\[\n\\|m\\|_2\\le M_n(m)\n\\le\\sum_{d=0}^n\\|m|_{\\mathbb N^d}\\|_2\n\\le\\sqrt{n+1}\\|m\\|_2,\n\\]\nand, for every \\(v\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\preceq v}m_t\\le M_n(m).\n\\]\nFor nonnegative forest arrays of finite total cost,\n\\[\nM(m+r)^2\\ge M(m)^2+M(r)^2.\n\\]\nThe costs commute with increasing limits of nonnegative arrays.</p>", "proof": "<p>Monotonicity and homogeneity follow by induction through levels. The same induction and the \\(\\ell_2\\) triangle inequality give subadditivity. Squaring the recurrence and discarding its nonnegative cross term proves the lower Hilbert bound.</p><p>The \\(\\ell_2\\)-norm of the \\(q_t\\) on level \\(d\\) is at most the norm of the \\(m_t\\) on that level plus the norm of the child values on level \\(d+1\\). Iterate and use Cauchy–Schwarz. Keeping only the child on the path to \\(v\\) proves the path estimate.</p><p>For the energy inequality, inductively show\n\\[\nq_t(m+r)\\ge\\bigl(q_t(m)^2+q_t(r)^2\\bigr)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(A,B\\) are the respective \\(\\ell_2\\)-norms of the child values, the induction gives\n\\[\nq_t(m+r)\\ge m_t+r_t+\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}\n\\ge\\sqrt{m_t^2+r_t^2}+\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}\n\\ge\\sqrt{(m_t+A)^2+(r_t+B)^2}.\n\\]\nSum the squares at the roots. Countably many children are handled by finite partial sums and monotone convergence; the same argument proves continuity under increasing limits.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition 8.2 and Lemma 8.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the segment-start quotient construction of Theorem 25, \\( \\frac{\\|x\\|_2}{n+1}\\le\\|x\\|_{S,n}\\le\\sqrt{n+1}\\|x\\|_2. \\) Thus the component norms and their outer \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum \\(X_S\\) are complete and reflexive, and …", "statement": "<p>For the segment-start quotient construction of Theorem 25,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|x\\|_2}{n+1}\\le\\|x\\|_{S,n}\\le\\sqrt{n+1}\\|x\\|_2.\n\\]\nThus the component norms and their outer \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum \\(X_S\\) are complete and reflexive, and finite support is dense. The forest norm equals the infimum of \\(M(m(a))\\) over forest representations.</p><p>For every node \\(v\\),\n\\[\n|x_v|\\le\\|x\\|_S.\n\\]\nEvery coordinate and root-to-node path has norm one. Finite ancestral projections are contractive, their complements have norm at most two, and fixed sibling sequences are weakly null.</p>", "proof": "<p>At output level \\(d\\), segments starting at one node \\(t\\) of level \\(h\\le d\\) contribute total absolute mass at most \\(m_t(a)\\). Distinct starts on level \\(h\\) have disjoint descendants. Hence this part of the output has Hilbert norm at most \\(\\|m(a)|_{\\mathbb N^h}\\|_2\\). Summing over \\(h\\le d\\), then over output levels, gives\n\\[\n\\|Pa\\|_2\\le(n+1)M_n(m(a)).\n\\]\nThe singleton representation \\(a_{tt}=x_t\\) has cost at most\n\\(\\sqrt{n+1}\\|x\\|_2\\). Homogeneity is immediate; adding representations and using Proposition 16 gives the triangle inequality. The comparisons imply definiteness, completeness, and reflexivity of each block.</p><p>The outer sum has dual the \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum of its component duals: restrictions and finite collections of approximate norming vectors give one norm inequality, Cauchy–Schwarz gives the other, and density determines the functional. Apply this twice for reflexivity. A forest representation bounds each component norm. Conversely choose component representations with costs at most\n\\(\\|x|_{T_n}\\|_{S,n}+\\eta2^{-n}\\) and apply Minkowski. Block and coordinate truncations prove density.</p><p>The absolute output at \\(v\\) is bounded by the sum of start masses on its ancestor path, hence by \\(M(m(a))\\). Singleton and root-path representations of cost one give equality for those vectors.</p><p>Restrict a representing segment to an ancestral set. It becomes empty or a segment with the same start, so start masses do not increase. Thus the head projection is contractive and its complement has norm at most two. Child vectors lie in one Hilbert-equivalent block and are weakly null there, and hence in \\(X_S\\).</p><p>A countable list of signed weights on identical segments may be used: combining entries can only decrease start masses; splitting a weight into pieces of its own sign preserves them. This permits the matching operations without attainment of the quotient infimum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Proposition 9.1, Lemma 9.2, and Lemma 9.4; also the unit-path calculation (89)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For the incomparable-segment norm of Theorem 27, let \\(Y_{I,h}\\) be the completion on \\(T_h\\).", "statement": "<p>For the incomparable-segment norm of Theorem 27, let \\(Y_{I,h}\\) be the completion on \\(T_h\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\|g\\|_2}{\\sqrt{h+1}}\\le\\|g\\|_{Y_{I,h}}\n\\le\\sqrt{h+1}\\|g\\|_2,\n\\qquad\nY_I=\\left(\\bigoplus_{h\\ge1}Y_{I,h}\\right)_{\\ell_2}.\n\\]\nBoth \\(Y_I\\) and \\(X_I=Y_I^*\\) are reflexive.</p><p>An atom is a represented vector\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^ma_iz_{S_i},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_i a_i^2\\le1,\\qquad (S_i)\\text{ incomparable}.\n\\]\nThe unit ball of \\(X_I\\) is the norm-closed convex hull of these atoms; in particular, coordinate evaluations span a dense subspace.</p><p>Every root path \\(v_s=z_{[\\varnothing_h,s]}\\) and coordinate \\(e_t\\) has norm one, and\n\\[\nv_{s^\\frown j}=v_s+e_{s^\\frown j},\n\\qquad e_{s^\\frown j}\\to0\\text{ weakly}.\n\\]</p><p>For finite prefix-closed \\(E\\), both \\(P_E,Q_E\\) are contractions on \\(Y_I\\) and \\(X_I\\). The projection \\(P_E\\) has finite rank, and every \\(x\\in X_I\\) can be approximated by such heads:\n\\[\n\\forall\\eta&gt;0\\ \\exists E\\quad \\|Q_Ex\\|_I&lt;\\eta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Singleton tests at any fixed level dominate that level's squared Hilbert norm. Summing over the \\(h+1\\) levels gives the lower comparison. For the upper comparison, each segment has at most \\(h+1\\) nodes, and the segments are disjoint, so\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\left|\\sum_{v\\in S_i}g(v)\\right|^2\n\\le(h+1)\\sum_i\\sum_{v\\in S_i}|g(v)|^2\n\\le(h+1)\\|g\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nTest families separate over components, and unions of finitely many component families are incomparable. This proves the \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum identity. Each block is Hilbert-equivalent and reflexive; the dual description of an \\(\\ell_2\\)-sum, proved by Cauchy–Schwarz and finite collections of nearly norming vectors, gives reflexivity of the sum and its dual.</p><p>Every atom has norm at most one and\n\\[\n\\sup_{A\\text{ atom}}A(g)=\\|g\\|_{Y_I}.\n\\]\nIf its closed convex hull missed a point of the dual ball, Hahn–Banach separation would give a separating functional in \\(X_I^*=Y_I\\), contradicting this identity. Every atom is finitely supported.</p><p>A root path is an atom of norm at most one; testing against a unit singleton at its terminal node gives the reverse inequality. The same proves \\(\\|e_t\\|_I=1\\). For \\(g\\in Y_I\\), child singleton tests give\n\\[\n\\sum_j|g(s^\\frown j)|^2\\le\\|g\\|_{Y_I}^2,\n\\]\nso child coordinates tend weakly to zero against all functionals, using reflexivity.</p><p>Intersect an incomparable segment family with \\(E\\), or take its portions outside \\(E\\). Prefix closure makes these initial and terminal segments, still incomparable. The resulting tests prove contractivity on \\(Y_I\\), first on finite arrays and then by completion. Adjoints act as the stated coordinate restrictions on the dense coordinate span of \\(X_I\\). The head range is finite dimensional. Approximate \\(x\\) by finite support, take its ancestor hull, and use contractivity of \\(Q_E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "the atom replacement and opposite-sign estimates (91)–(94) in the proof of Theorem 9.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(E\\) be finite and prefix closed, and let \\(f\\in Y_I\\) be supported on \\(E\\), with \\(\\|f\\|_{Y_I}\\le1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be finite and prefix closed, and let \\(f\\in Y_I\\) be supported on \\(E\\), with \\(\\|f\\|_{Y_I}\\le1\\). For an atom\n\\[\nA=\\sum_i a_iz_{S_i},\n\\]\nput\n\\[\np_i^A=z_{S_i}(f),\\qquad\nd(A)=1-A(f),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat A=\\sum_i p_i^Az_{S_i\\setminus E}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_i(a_i-p_i^A)^2\\le2d(A),\\qquad\n1-\\sum_i(p_i^A)^2\\le2d(A),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\widehat A-Q_EA\\|_I\\le\\sqrt{2d(A)}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be the minimal nodes outside \\(E\\). Write\n\\[\n\\widehat A=\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal R}r_q^Az_{[q,t_q^A]}.\n\\]\nFor two represented atoms \\(A,A'\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\{q:r_q^Ar_q^{A'}&lt;0\\}}|r_q^Ar_q^{A'}|\n\\le\\frac12\\left(\n1-\\sum_i(p_i^A)^2+\n1-\\sum_i(p_i^{A'})^2\n\\right)\n\\le d(A)+d(A').\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Both vectors \\((a_i)\\) and \\((p_i^A)\\) have Euclidean norm at most one. Expand\n\\[\n2d(A)=\\sum_i(a_i-p_i^A)^2+\n\\left(1-\\sum_ia_i^2\\right)+\n\\left(1-\\sum_i(p_i^A)^2\\right).\n\\]\nAll terms on the right are nonnegative. The nonempty terminal pieces \\(S_i\\setminus E\\) remain incomparable, so the atom norm bound gives the replacement estimate.</p><p>If \\(p_i^A\\ne0\\) and \\(S_i\\setminus E\\ne\\varnothing\\), the original segment meets \\(E\\) and exits through exactly one \\(q\\in\\mathcal R\\). At most one segment of an incomparable family can contain that \\(q\\). Its coefficient \\(r_q^A\\) is the sum of \\(f\\) from the original start \\(s_q^A\\in E\\) to the predecessor of \\(q\\). This gives the displayed exit representation.</p><p>For opposite coefficients, \\(s_q^A,s_q^{A'}\\) lie on the same ancestor path and are distinct. For exits with \\(s_q^A\\prec s_q^{A'}\\), shorten the corresponding segment of \\(A\\) to end just before \\(s_q^{A'}\\). These are nonempty subsegments, and all such shortenings preserve incomparability. The squared evaluation increases from \\((r_q^A)^2\\) to\n\\((r_q^A-r_q^{A'})^2\\), an increase at least \\(2|r_q^Ar_q^{A'}|\\). The sum of all modified squared evaluations is at most one. Their products therefore total at most\n\\(\\tfrac12(1-\\sum_i(p_i^A)^2)\\).\nFor the opposite ordering shorten the segments of \\(A'\\). Add the two estimates, and use the defect bounds.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 32, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [2, 5, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 9, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [29, 0, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [30, 0, "cite"], [30, 1, "cite"], [30, 24, "cite"], [30, 26, "cite"], [34, 2, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 2, "cite"], [35, 9, "cite"], [36, 3, "cite"], [37, 5, "cite"], [38, 6, "cite"], [38, 7, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 10, "cite"], [40, 13, "cite"], [40, 42, "cite"], [41, 14, "cite"], [41, 16, "cite"], [41, 18, "cite"], [41, 20, "cite"], [41, 22, "cite"], [41, 44, "cite"], [42, 14, "cite"], [42, 16, "cite"], [42, 44, "cite"], [43, 23, "cite"], [44, 43, "cite"], [45, 24, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 24, "cite"], [47, 26, "cite"], [47, 27, "cite"], [47, 28, "cite"], [48, 27, "cite"]]});
